S3 
 3  The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler 

 
 3.1  Overview of the construction 

 
 We first describe the Proximal BPS at a high level.  Figure   1  gives a geometric overview. 
 
  
 Figure 1:  Geometric overview of Proximal BPS.
(a) The initial law is lifted to the augmented distribution. At each
iteration, (b) the auxiliary point is reflected through the current
position, and (c) a conditional reference point is drawn. With
probability  \rho , that point becomes the next position; with
probability  1-\rho , it determines the equilibrium position
 c=Y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X})  of the deterministic harmonic flow
for the time- \pi  BPS half-turn process in (d), which starts from the current
position. 
 
 
 Baseline Gibbs update and reflection of the auxiliary variable. 

 
 The usual proximal sampler for the augmentation
( 2.6 ) is the two-block Gibbs update 
 

 
  
 Y^{+}\sim\mathcal{N}(x,\eta I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=Y^{+}}\,. 
  
 
 Each update discards the previous value of the coordinate it changes:
the first adds fresh Gaussian noise to  x , while the second makes an
independent conditional draw using a restricted Gaussian oracle (RGO).
The latter conditional law is local and well-conditioned by
( 2.10 ), which makes the RGO inexpensive for
small  \eta . The same localization, however, generally requires more
iterations, so  \eta  balances the cost of each conditional
update against the number of iterations. This construction and its RGO
implementation are studied in
 [ 6 ,  9 ,  15 ,  17 ] . 
 
 
 Proximal BPS preserves the same augmented target but replaces this
two-block Gibbs transition with one that retains dependence on the
preceding augmented state. This memory plays the role of momentum and
underlies the kinetic acceleration established below. For the
 Y -update, the fresh Gaussian draw is replaced by the deterministic
reflection (see Figure  1 (b)) 
 

 
  
 y^{+}=2x-y\,,\hskip 20.00003pty^{+}-x=-(y-x)\,. 
  
 
 This map preserves  \pi_{\eta} , because it negates the centered Gaussian
residual  Y-X\mid X  without changing its conditional law. Consequently, the residual from the current augmented
state is carried into the next position update rather than discarded and
replaced by fresh Gaussian noise. The  X -update described next also
retains dependence on the current position along the dominant branch,
which runs the half-turn process; only occasional conditional resampling
discards this memory. 
 
 
 
 Conditional reference point and occasional resampling. 

 
 After the reflection sets  Y=y^{+} , Proximal BPS updates  X  using a
mixture of two kernels that preserve
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}} : independent conditional resampling and the
kernel induced by the conditional half-turn process defined below. Draw 
 

 
  
 \widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}=\begin{cases}\widetilde{X}\,,&\text{with probability }\rho\quad\text{(conditional resampling)}\,,\\
x_{\pi}\,,&\text{with probability }1-\rho\quad\text{(half-turn process, defined below)}\,.\end{cases} 
  
 
 Choosing  \rho 
small makes the half-turn process the dominant option,
while conditional resampling prevents periodicity.
When conditional resampling is selected, the RGO output  \widetilde{X} 
is the next position. On the dominant branch, the process uses
 \widetilde{X}  as an auxiliary reference point, and its output  x_{\pi} 
remains dependent on the current position.
 Theorem   3.5  shows that composing this update with the
reflection  y^{+}=2x-y  accelerates convergence and thus reduces the
iteration complexity, as the
updates carry information across iterations. 
 
 
 
 The conditional half-turn process. 

 
 This process updates  X  through a kernel that preserves
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}  while retaining dependence on the current
position, rather than replacing  x  by an independent conditional
draw. For this purpose, we construct a stochastic process on phase
space  (x,p)  with invariant law
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I) . Each run starts from
 x_{0}=x  and an independent  p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I) . This momentum is
temporary: it is discarded at the end of the process, whereas the memory
across iterations remains in  (X,Y) . 
 
 
 At fixed  Y=y^{+} , vanilla BPS would use straight-line motion and bounce against the full gradient of
 V(\cdot)+\|{\cdot}-y^{+}\|^{2}/(2\eta) . Although exact, this does not exploit the fact that we
know the quadratic coupling potential exactly, which dominates  V  when  \eta  is
small. We thus incorporate this quadratic term into the deterministic
motion between bounce events, which account for  V . 
 
 
 A direct version of this idea would still bounce against the full
gradient  \nabla V(x) , whose magnitude need not be small even inside
the conditional region localized near  y^{+} .
This is where the conditional reference point  \widetilde{X}  is used.
The fixed gradient  \nabla V(\widetilde{X})  is incorporated into the
deterministic motion, whose equilibrium position is
 c\coloneqq y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X}) . Bounces handle only
 \nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X}) , which makes the bounce rate small
due to smoothness of the potential. 
 
 
 Ignoring bounces, the harmonic motion traces a periodic orbit around
 c  and returns to its initial point after time  2\pi . By a
 half-turn  we mean stopping halfway around this orbit, at time
 \pi . See Figure  1 (d). This midpoint is chosen to preserve memory. The temporary
momentum is needed to define invariant phase space dynamics, but it
should not replace the information carried by the current position.
If we stop after a quarter-period, the new position
would be determined by the freshly drawn momentum rather than by
 x_{0} . Continuing through a full period would undo the move and
return to  x_{0} . The half-period is the first non-trivial stopping time
that avoids both failures: the fresh momentum no longer contributes to
the position, and the displacement from  c  is retained with its
direction reversed. Thus the no-bounce map is 
 

 
  
 (x_{\pi},p_{\pi})=(2c-x_{0},-p_{0})\,. 
  
 
 The BPS bounces correct for  V  along the trajectory, but the process
keeps this time- \pi  horizon so that its dominant harmonic motion
retains the same memory-preserving geometry. 
 
 
 The rest of this section formalizes the construction. We reserve
 t\in[0,\pi]  for the phase space process  (x_{t},p_{t})  during one run
of the half-turn process
and  k  for the Markov chain  (X_{k},Y_{k}) .
 Section   3.2  constructs the conditional half-turn process
and proves reversibility of its induced kernel;
 Section   3.3  defines the full ideal
transition. The practical gradient-only
implementation is discussed in  Section   4 . 
 
 
 
 
 3.2  The ideal conditional half-turn process 

 
 Fix  Y=y . Recall from ( 2.8 ) that the position
 X  to be sampled has conditional law 
 

 
  
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\propto e^{-V_{y}(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\,,\hskip 20.00003ptV_{y}(x)\coloneqq V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\,. 
  
 
 We construct the half-turn process so that its induced transition
preserves this law. The
construction starts from vanilla BPS for the conditional target, absorbs
the quadratic coupling potential into the deterministic motion, and then
introduces
an independent conditional reference point to localize the remaining
bounce rate. We begin by introducing a Gaussian momentum
 p\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I) . The phase space law to be preserved is 
 

 
  
 \nu_{\eta,y}(\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p)\coloneqq\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\,\mathcal{N}(0,I)(\mathrm{d}p)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V_{y}(x)-\frac{\|p\|^{2}}{2}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p\,. 
  
 (3.1) 
 
 Recall from ( 2.4 ) that  R_{h}  denotes specular
reflection across the hyperplane orthogonal to  h . 
 
 
 A natural first attempt is to apply vanilla BPS directly to this
conditional distribution. Its infinitesimal generator acts on a smooth
test function  f  as 
 

 
  
 \begin{split}(\mathsf{L}_{y}^{\rm vanilla}f)(x,p)&=\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)\\
&\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}\nabla V_{y}(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{\nabla V_{y}(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split} 
  
 (3.2) 
 
 The common factor  \sqrt{\eta}  only fixes the unit of time; it does not
change the invariant law. We choose this unit so that the harmonic motion
introduced below has period  2\pi .
The first term gives straight-line motion, while the second reflects the
momentum against the full conditional gradient. This process is exact,
but it can be expensive: even when  V=0 , the quadratic coupling potential
 \|{\cdot}-y\|^{2}/(2\eta)  produces a stationary mean bounce rate of order
 \sqrt{d} . 
 
 
 To reduce the number of bounce events, we put the quadratic part of the
conditional gradient into the deterministic dynamics. This gives the
generator 
 

 
  
 \begin{split}(\mathsf{L}_{y}^{\rm harm}f)(x,p)&=\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\frac{(x-y)^{\top}}{\sqrt{\eta}}\nabla_{p}f(x,p)\\
&\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}\nabla V(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{\nabla V(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split} 
  
 (3.3) 
 
 The first line is the Hamiltonian flow associated with the quadratic
coupling potential: between bounces, it rotates  (x-y)/\sqrt{\eta}  and
 p . This use of a quadratic reference flow, with the remaining force
handled by bounce events, is analogous to the Boomerang
sampler  [ 4 ] .
The direct harmonic generator ( 3.3 )
still bounces against the full gradient  \nabla V , so its bounce rate
can remain large. 
 
 
 To further reduce the bounce rate and the resulting computational
cost, we introduce a conditional reference point and use the smoothness of
 V  to control the local gradient difference. Specifically, draw
 \widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}  independently and write the exact
decomposition 
 

 
  
 \nabla V(x)=\nabla V(\widetilde{X})+\{\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\}\,. 
  
 
 Conditionally on  \widetilde{X}=\widetilde{x} , we incorporate the fixed
reference term into the deterministic dynamics and retain only the gradient
difference in the BPS jump term. Define the equilibrium position of this
deterministic harmonic flow and the residual gradient by 
 

 
  
 c_{y,\widetilde{x}}\coloneqq y-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\,,\hskip 20.00003pth_{\widetilde{x}}(x)\coloneqq\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\,. 
  
 (3.4) 
 
 The resulting generator is 
 

 
  
 \begin{split}\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{x}}f(x,p)&\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\frac{(x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})^{\top}}{\sqrt{\eta}}\nabla_{p}f(x,p)\\
&\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split} 
  
 (3.5) 
 
 The harmonic and residual terms sum to the full conditional force
 \sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V_{y}(x) . Thus  \widetilde{x}  changes only how this
force is divided between deterministic motion and bounces. By the
 \beta -smoothness of  V , 
 

 
  
 \|h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)\|=\|\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\|\leq\beta\,\|x-\widetilde{x}\|\,. 
  
 
 Drawing the reference point from the same localized conditional law keeps
the residual gradient small and reduces the expected number of bounces per
run of the half-turn process to order  \beta\eta\sqrt{d} ; see
 Proposition   3.3 . 
 
 
 Having specified the dynamics, it remains to choose the integration
horizon. For fixed  (y,\widetilde{x}) , the deterministic part of
( 3.5 ) is harmonic motion about its equilibrium
position  c_{y,\widetilde{x}} . In the absence of bounces, its position at
time  t  satisfies 
 

 
  
 x_{t}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}}=(x_{0}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\cos t+\sqrt{\eta}\,p_{0}\sin t\,. 
  
 (3.6) 
 
 As discussed earlier, we choose the integration horizon  t=\pi , corresponding to one
half-period. This is the first non-zero harmonic time at which the
position  x_{\pi}  no longer depends on the initial momentum  p_{0} .
Without bounces, the resulting map is 
 

 
  
 (x_{\pi},p_{\pi})=(2c_{y,\widetilde{x}}-x_{0},-p_{0})\,. 
  
 (3.7) 
 
 Thus, the position is reflected through the equilibrium position
 c_{y,\widetilde{x}}  and retains its dependence on  x_{0} . Bounce
events correct for the residual force along the trajectory, while the
fixed horizon  t=\pi  retains the memory-preserving structure of the
half-turn process. 
 
 
 The construction is now complete. The following algorithm gives its
pathwise prescription: it draws the reference point and momentum, evolves
the process with generator ( 3.5 ) for time  \pi , and
returns only the final position. 
 
 
 Algorithm 1    Simulation of the ideal conditional half-turn process 
 
 
               1: 
              
              
             Input:  state  (x,y) 

 
 
               2: 
              
              
            Draw  \widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}  and
 p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)  independently.

 
 
               3: 
              
              
            Query  \nabla V(\widetilde{X}) .

 
 
               4: 
              
              
            Set  x_{0}\leftarrow x .

 
 
               5: 
              
              
            Evolve  (x_{t},p_{t})  according to

 

 
  
 \dot{x}=\sqrt{\eta}\,p\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\dot{p}=-\frac{x-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}-\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\,, 
  
 (3.8) 
 until  t=\pi .

 
 
               6: 
              
              
            During the evolution, generate bounce events at rate

 

 
  
 \lambda_{\widetilde{X}}(x,p)=\sqrt{\eta}\,\bigl[p^{\top}[\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})]\bigr]_{+}\,. 
  
 (3.9) 
 
 
 
               7: 
              
              
            At each bounce, set
 p_{t}\leftarrow R_{\nabla V(x_{t})-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})}p_{t} .

 
 
               8: 
              
              
             return   x_{\pi} 

 
 
 
 
 Proposition 3.1   (Well-posedness and stationarity of the conditional phase space process) . 
 
 For every fixed  y,\widetilde{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}  and initial phase space point
 (x_{0},p_{0}) , the event-driven dynamics in  Algorithm   1 , with
the reference point fixed at  \widetilde{x} , defines a unique
non-explosive, time-homogeneous Markov process. The phase space measure
 \nu_{\eta,y}  is stationary for this process. In particular, the
returned position  x_{\pi}  is well-defined almost surely. 
 
 
 
 The well-posedness and stationarity assertions are proved in
 Section   A.1 . 
 
 
 The induced Markov kernel on positions also satisfies the following
reversibility property. 
 
 
 Proposition 3.2   (Reversibility of the conditional half-turn kernel) . 
 
 For every  y\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , let  \mathcal{H}_{y}  be the Markov kernel on
 \mathbb{R}^{d}  induced by  Algorithm   1 ; thus
 \mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\cdot)  is the law of the returned position  x_{\pi} 
for input  (x,y) . Then  \mathcal{H}_{y}  is reversible with respect to
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} . 
 
 
 
 The proof is deferred to Appendix  A.2 . 
 
 
 The following proposition quantifies the stationary bounce count of the
ideal half-turn process. 
 
 
 Proposition 3.3   (Stationary bounce count) . 
 
 There exists a universal constant  K_{\rm id}\geq 1  such that the
following holds. Suppose that  (x_{0},Y)\sim\pi_{\eta} . Conditional on
 Y , let  \widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y}  be independent of
 x_{0} , and let  p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)  be independent of all position
variables. If  N_{\rm b}^{\rm id}  denotes the number of bounce events
in the resulting ideal half-turn process of  Algorithm   1 , then 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}N_{\rm b}^{\rm id}\leq K_{\rm id}\beta\eta\sqrt{d}\,. 
  
 (3.10) 
 
 
 
 
 The proof is deferred to
 Appendix  A ,
proof of  Proposition   3.3 . 
 
 
 
 3.3  Ideal Proximal BPS 

 
 With the conditional half-turn kernel in hand, we can now define the ideal
Proximal BPS. For a resampling probability  \rho\in(0,1) , specified in  Theorem   3.5 , its
augmented-state transition kernel is 
 

 
  
 \begin{split}\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)&\coloneqq\delta_{2x-y}(\mathrm{d}y^{\prime})\,\bigl[\rho\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})+(1-\rho)\,\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\bigr]\,.\end{split} 
  
 (3.11) 
 
 The Dirac factor enforces  y^{\prime}=2x-y ; the two terms in brackets are
conditional resampling and the half-turn kernel. Thus
( 3.11 ) is the formal version of the update
displayed in  Section   3.1 . The following algorithm
gives an equivalent simulation. 
 
 
 Algorithm 2    Ideal Proximal BPS 
 
 
               1: 
              
              
             Input:   (X_{0},Y_{0}) ,  T , and  \rho 

 
 
               2: 
              
              
             for   k=0,\ldots,T-1   do 

 
 
               3: 
              
              
               Y_{k+1}\leftarrow 2X_{k}-Y_{k} 

 
 
               4: 
              
              
              Draw  B_{k}\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\rho) 

 
 
               5: 
              
              
               if   B_{k}=1   then 

 
 
               6: 
              
              
               Draw
 X_{k+1}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=Y_{k+1}}  exactly

 
 
               7: 
              
              
               else 
 
 
               8: 
              
              
                X_{k+1}\leftarrow\textsc{IdealHalfTurn}(X_{k},Y_{k+1}) 
using  Algorithm   1 

 
 
               9: 
              
              
               end   if 
 
 
               10: 
              
              
             end   for 
 
 
               11: 
              
              
             return   X_{T} 

 
 
 
 
 Proposition 3.4   (Invariance of the ideal Proximal BPS) . 
 
 The transition kernel  \mathcal{K}  in
( 3.11 ) preserves the augmented law  \pi_{\eta} . 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 The reflection preserves  \pi_{\eta}  by  Proposition   2.1 .
At fixed  y^{\prime} , the first term in brackets in
( 3.11 ) preserves
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}  by definition, and the second does so by
 Proposition   3.2 . The conditional mixture, and hence its
composition with the reflection, preserves  \pi_{\eta} .
∎ 
 
 
 
 The next theorem gives the quantitative convergence guarantee for
ideal Proximal BPS and also underlies the implementable guarantee
in  Section   4 . Its proof is in
Appendices  B  and  C . 
 
 
 Theorem 3.5   ( \chi^{2}  convergence) . 
 
 There exist universal constants
 c_{0},\rho_{\star}\in(0,1)  and  C\geq 1  such that
the following holds. Assume ( 1.1 ), and let
 0<\eta\leq c_{0}/\beta .
Set 
 

 
  
 \ell_{\eta}\coloneqq\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\rho\coloneqq\rho_{\star}\sqrt{\alpha\eta\,\ell_{\eta}}\in(0,1/2]\,, 
  
 (3.12) 
 
 and let  \mathcal{K}  be the transition kernel in
( 3.11 ) with this choice of  \rho .
Then, for all integers  N  satisfying 
 

 
  
 N\geq\frac{C}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,, 
  
 (3.13) 
 
 all probability measures  \nu_{0}  on
 \mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}  satisfying
 \chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})<\infty , and all integers  J\geq 0 , 
 

 
  
 \chi^{2}\bigl(\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN}\|\pi_{\eta}\bigr)\leq e^{-J}\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\,\,. 
  
 (3.14) 
 
 Consequently, the  X -marginal satisfies 
 

 
  
 \operatorname{TV}\bigl((\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN})^{X},\mu\bigr)\leq\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})}\exp\Bigl\{-\frac{J}{2}\Bigr\}\,. 
  
 (3.15) 
 
 
 
 
 Theorem   3.5  is a hypocoercive estimate. To see this,
let  f\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})  have mean zero and write 
 

 
  
 \bar{f}(y)\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[f(X,Y)\mid Y=y]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\widetilde{f}(x,y)\coloneqq f(x,y)-\bar{f}(y)\,. 
  
 
 Then,  \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[\widetilde{f}(X,Y)\mid Y]=0  and
 \|f\|_{2}^{2}=\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}+\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2} , where
 \|\cdot\|_{2}  denotes the norm in  L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) . At fixed
 Y , conditional resampling removes  \widetilde{f} , whereas the
conditional half-turn leaves  \bar{f}  unchanged and does not increase
 \|\widetilde{f}\|_{2} . Since reflection preserves the  L^{2}  norm,
the one-step Markov operator  \mathsf{K}  satisfies 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{K}f\|_{2}^{2}-\|f\|_{2}^{2}\leq-\rho\,\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2}\,. 
  
 
 The ordinary  L^{2}  estimate therefore gives no decay when
 f=\bar{f} . 
 
 
 It remains to understand observables that depend only on  Y . For
 f=\bar{f} , reflection changes  \bar{f}(Y)  into
 \bar{f}(2X-Y) , which can be decomposed as 
 

 
  
 \bar{f}(2X-Y)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[\bar{f}(2X-Y)\mid Y]+r_{f}(X,Y)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[r_{f}(X,Y)\mid Y]=0\,. 
  
 
 The first term still depends only on  Y , whereas  r_{f}  averages to
zero when  X  is resampled conditionally on  Y . Since  f  is
centered, so is  \bar{f} , and  Lemma   B.1  shows
that the squared norm of the first term is at most
 (1-c\alpha\eta)\,\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}  for a universal constant  c>0 .
Reflection nevertheless preserves the total norm: the decrease
of the first component is balanced by the contribution from  r_{f} .
Thus the usual  L^{2}  norm sees only a redistribution between the two
components, even though conditional resampling contracts the second one.
This interaction is the hypocoercive contraction
mechanism  [ 14 ,  21 ] . 
 
 
 To capture this mechanism, we add a corrector to the ordinary
squared  L^{2}  norm and use the modified Lyapunov function 
 

 
  
 \mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\coloneqq\|f\|_{2}^{2}+\omega\mathscr{C}(f)\,. 
  
 
 The corrector is chosen so that its one-step change contributes a negative
term proportional to  \|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2} , together with terms controlled by
 \|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2} . Combined with the direct  L^{2}  estimate
above, this gives control of both components. The model-specific input
for the half-turn is  Proposition   B.2 , which bounds its
contribution to the change of the corrector.
This is a discrete version of the modified- L^{2}  method of Dolbeault,
Mouhot, and Schmeiser  [ 14 ]  (see also the sharpened formulation of Fan, Li, and
Lu  [ 16 ] ). 
 
 
 In the appendices, we denote by  \mathsf{P}  the
conditional expectation given  Y  and set  \mathsf{P}^{\perp}\coloneqq I-\mathsf{P} , and let
 \mathsf{U}  denote pullback by the reflection  Y\mapsto 2X-Y . Then 
 

 
  
 f_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}f=\bar{f}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptf_{\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f=\widetilde{f}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptr_{f}=\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}f\,. 
  
 
 The proof steps are organized as follows. Appendix  B.1 
sets up the decomposition above and derives the ordinary  L^{2}  estimate.
Appendix  B.2  states the two estimates
used to recover control of  f_{\mathsf{P}} . The reflection estimate is proved
in Appendix  C.1 , and the half-turn estimate in
Appendices  C.2 – C.3 .
Finally, Appendix  B.3  constructs the modified energy, proves
its contraction, chooses  \rho  and the block length, and converts the
result into the stated  \chi^{2}  and total variation bounds. 
 
 
 Remark 3.6 . 
 
 The use of  L^{2}  analysis is partially motivated by  [ 23 ] , which gave obstructions to entropic convergence for piecewise-deterministic Markov processes such as the BPS. 
 
 
 

S3.SS1 
 3.1  Overview of the construction 

 
 We first describe the Proximal BPS at a high level.  Figure   1  gives a geometric overview. 
 
  
 Figure 1:  Geometric overview of Proximal BPS.
(a) The initial law is lifted to the augmented distribution. At each
iteration, (b) the auxiliary point is reflected through the current
position, and (c) a conditional reference point is drawn. With
probability  \rho , that point becomes the next position; with
probability  1-\rho , it determines the equilibrium position
 c=Y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X})  of the deterministic harmonic flow
for the time- \pi  BPS half-turn process in (d), which starts from the current
position. 
 
 
 Baseline Gibbs update and reflection of the auxiliary variable. 

 
 The usual proximal sampler for the augmentation
( 2.6 ) is the two-block Gibbs update 
 

 
  
 Y^{+}\sim\mathcal{N}(x,\eta I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=Y^{+}}\,. 
  
 
 Each update discards the previous value of the coordinate it changes:
the first adds fresh Gaussian noise to  x , while the second makes an
independent conditional draw using a restricted Gaussian oracle (RGO).
The latter conditional law is local and well-conditioned by
( 2.10 ), which makes the RGO inexpensive for
small  \eta . The same localization, however, generally requires more
iterations, so  \eta  balances the cost of each conditional
update against the number of iterations. This construction and its RGO
implementation are studied in
 [ 6 ,  9 ,  15 ,  17 ] . 
 
 
 Proximal BPS preserves the same augmented target but replaces this
two-block Gibbs transition with one that retains dependence on the
preceding augmented state. This memory plays the role of momentum and
underlies the kinetic acceleration established below. For the
 Y -update, the fresh Gaussian draw is replaced by the deterministic
reflection (see Figure  1 (b)) 
 

 
  
 y^{+}=2x-y\,,\hskip 20.00003pty^{+}-x=-(y-x)\,. 
  
 
 This map preserves  \pi_{\eta} , because it negates the centered Gaussian
residual  Y-X\mid X  without changing its conditional law. Consequently, the residual from the current augmented
state is carried into the next position update rather than discarded and
replaced by fresh Gaussian noise. The  X -update described next also
retains dependence on the current position along the dominant branch,
which runs the half-turn process; only occasional conditional resampling
discards this memory. 
 
 
 
 Conditional reference point and occasional resampling. 

 
 After the reflection sets  Y=y^{+} , Proximal BPS updates  X  using a
mixture of two kernels that preserve
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}} : independent conditional resampling and the
kernel induced by the conditional half-turn process defined below. Draw 
 

 
  
 \widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}=\begin{cases}\widetilde{X}\,,&\text{with probability }\rho\quad\text{(conditional resampling)}\,,\\
x_{\pi}\,,&\text{with probability }1-\rho\quad\text{(half-turn process, defined below)}\,.\end{cases} 
  
 
 Choosing  \rho 
small makes the half-turn process the dominant option,
while conditional resampling prevents periodicity.
When conditional resampling is selected, the RGO output  \widetilde{X} 
is the next position. On the dominant branch, the process uses
 \widetilde{X}  as an auxiliary reference point, and its output  x_{\pi} 
remains dependent on the current position.
 Theorem   3.5  shows that composing this update with the
reflection  y^{+}=2x-y  accelerates convergence and thus reduces the
iteration complexity, as the
updates carry information across iterations. 
 
 
 
 The conditional half-turn process. 

 
 This process updates  X  through a kernel that preserves
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}  while retaining dependence on the current
position, rather than replacing  x  by an independent conditional
draw. For this purpose, we construct a stochastic process on phase
space  (x,p)  with invariant law
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I) . Each run starts from
 x_{0}=x  and an independent  p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I) . This momentum is
temporary: it is discarded at the end of the process, whereas the memory
across iterations remains in  (X,Y) . 
 
 
 At fixed  Y=y^{+} , vanilla BPS would use straight-line motion and bounce against the full gradient of
 V(\cdot)+\|{\cdot}-y^{+}\|^{2}/(2\eta) . Although exact, this does not exploit the fact that we
know the quadratic coupling potential exactly, which dominates  V  when  \eta  is
small. We thus incorporate this quadratic term into the deterministic
motion between bounce events, which account for  V . 
 
 
 A direct version of this idea would still bounce against the full
gradient  \nabla V(x) , whose magnitude need not be small even inside
the conditional region localized near  y^{+} .
This is where the conditional reference point  \widetilde{X}  is used.
The fixed gradient  \nabla V(\widetilde{X})  is incorporated into the
deterministic motion, whose equilibrium position is
 c\coloneqq y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X}) . Bounces handle only
 \nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X}) , which makes the bounce rate small
due to smoothness of the potential. 
 
 
 Ignoring bounces, the harmonic motion traces a periodic orbit around
 c  and returns to its initial point after time  2\pi . By a
 half-turn  we mean stopping halfway around this orbit, at time
 \pi . See Figure  1 (d). This midpoint is chosen to preserve memory. The temporary
momentum is needed to define invariant phase space dynamics, but it
should not replace the information carried by the current position.
If we stop after a quarter-period, the new position
would be determined by the freshly drawn momentum rather than by
 x_{0} . Continuing through a full period would undo the move and
return to  x_{0} . The half-period is the first non-trivial stopping time
that avoids both failures: the fresh momentum no longer contributes to
the position, and the displacement from  c  is retained with its
direction reversed. Thus the no-bounce map is 
 

 
  
 (x_{\pi},p_{\pi})=(2c-x_{0},-p_{0})\,. 
  
 
 The BPS bounces correct for  V  along the trajectory, but the process
keeps this time- \pi  horizon so that its dominant harmonic motion
retains the same memory-preserving geometry. 
 
 
 The rest of this section formalizes the construction. We reserve
 t\in[0,\pi]  for the phase space process  (x_{t},p_{t})  during one run
of the half-turn process
and  k  for the Markov chain  (X_{k},Y_{k}) .
 Section   3.2  constructs the conditional half-turn process
and proves reversibility of its induced kernel;
 Section   3.3  defines the full ideal
transition. The practical gradient-only
implementation is discussed in  Section   4 . 
 
 

