S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1 
 Choice of parameters. 

 
 Given  \Delta\geq 1 ,  \varepsilon\in(0,1/4) ,
 0<\eta\leq 1/\beta , and a tuning constant  K\geq 1 , define 
 

 
  
 \ell\coloneqq K\,\Bigl[\Delta+\log\Bigl(\frac{Kd\kappa}{\varepsilon\alpha\eta}\Bigr)\Bigr]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\bar{\lambda}_{\rm impl}\coloneqq K\beta\eta\,(\sqrt{d\ell}+\ell)\,. 
  
 (4.8) 
 
 Set  \rho  as in ( 3.12 ), let  N  be the smallest
integer satisfying ( 3.13 ), and define 
 
 
 
  
 \displaystyle T 
 \displaystyle\coloneqq\Bigl\lceil K\,\Bigl(\Delta+\log\frac{4}{\varepsilon}\Bigr)\Bigr\rceil\,N\,,\hskip 20.00003pta\coloneqq\frac{\varepsilon}{6T}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptQ_{\rm prox}\coloneqq\bigl\lceil K\log(\kappa\ell)\bigr\rceil\,. 
  
 (4.9) 
 
 
 
 Theorem 4.3   (Sampler from an order-2 Rényi warm start) . 
 
 There exist universal constants  c\in(0,1)  and  K\geq 1  such
that the following holds. Assume ( 1.1 ). Let
 \Delta\geq 1 ,  \varepsilon\in(0,1/4) , and let  \mu_{0}  be a
probability measure on  \mathbb{R}^{d}  satisfying
 D_{2}(\mu_{0}\|\mu)\leq\Delta , and choose  0<\eta\leq 1/\beta .
Assume that 
 

 
  
 \beta\eta\,\bigl(\sqrt{d\ell}+\ell\bigr)\leq c\,. 
  
 (4.10) 
 
 Run  Algorithm   2  from  X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}  using
( 4.8 )–( 4.9 ). If
 \widehat{X}  is its output and  N_{\nabla V}  is its total number
of gradient queries, then 
 

 
  
 \operatorname{TV}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(\widehat{X}),\mu\bigr)\leq\varepsilon\,. 
  
 (4.11) 
 
 Moreover, its expected query count satisfies 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}N_{\nabla V}\leq\frac{K}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,\Bigl(\Delta+\log\frac{4}{\varepsilon}\Bigr)\,\bigl[Q_{\rm prox}+\beta\eta\,\bigl(\sqrt{d\ell}+\ell\bigr)\bigr]\,. 
  
 (4.12) 
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 Set  \nu_{0}\coloneqq\mathcal{L}(X_{0},Y_{0}) . By
( 2.18 ) and ( 2.16 ), 
 

 
  
 D_{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\leq\Delta\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\leq e^{\Delta}\,. 
  
 
 Since  \ell\geq 1 , ( 4.10 ) gives
 \beta\eta\sqrt{d}\leq c . Taking  c\leq c_{0}  and using
( 3.15 ), 
 

 
  
 \operatorname{TV}\bigl((\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{T})^{X},\mu\bigr)\leq\frac{1}{2}\exp\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\,\Bigl[\Delta-\Bigl\lceil K\,\Bigl(\Delta+\log\frac{4}{\varepsilon}\Bigr)\Bigr\rceil\Bigr]\Bigr\}\leq\frac{\varepsilon}{2}\,, 
  
 
 after increasing  K . Minimality of  N , together with
 \alpha\eta\leq 1 , gives 
 
 
 
  
 \displaystyle N 
 \displaystyle\leq 1+\frac{C}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\leq\frac{C+1}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,, 
  
 (4.13) 
 
  
 \displaystyle T 
 \displaystyle\leq\frac{C}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,\Bigl(\Delta+\log\frac{4}{\varepsilon}\Bigr)\,. 
  
 
 
 
 Let  \nu_{k}\coloneqq\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{k} , and let  \nu_{k}^{+}  be its image
under the reflection at the start of transition  k . Since
 \mathcal{K}  preserves  \pi_{\eta}  and the reflection is a
 \pi_{\eta} -preserving bijection, data processing gives 
 

 
  
 D_{2}(\nu_{k}^{+}\|\pi_{\eta})=D_{2}(\nu_{k}\|\pi_{\eta})\leq D_{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\leq\Delta\,,\hskip 20.00003pt0\leq k<T\,. 
  
 
 Since  a^{-1}=6T/\varepsilon , ( 4.13 ) and
( 4.6 ) yield, for a sufficiently large universal  K , 
 

 
  
 0<a<\frac{1}{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\log\frac{1}{a}\vee\ell_{a,\Delta}\leq C\,\Bigl[\Delta+\log\Bigl(\frac{Kd\kappa}{\varepsilon\alpha\eta}\Bigr)\Bigr]\leq\ell\,. 
  
 (4.14) 
 
 Taking
 c\leq\min\{c_{0},c_{\rm evt},K_{\rm RGO}^{-1}\}  and
 K\geq K_{\rm evt} , we have 
 

 
  
 \begin{split}&K_{\rm RGO}\beta\eta\,\bigl\{\sqrt{d\log(1/a)}+\log(1/a)\bigr\}\leq K_{\rm RGO}c\leq 1\,,\\
&\bar{\lambda}_{\rm impl}=K\beta\eta\,(\sqrt{d\ell}+\ell)\geq K_{\rm evt}\beta\eta\,(\sqrt{d\ell_{a,\Delta}}+\ell_{a,\Delta})=\bar{\lambda}_{a,\Delta}\,.\end{split} 
  
 
 Thus,  Theorem   4.1  applies with  \delta=a , while
 Proposition   4.2  and monotonicity in the cap apply along every
ideal transition. If
 p_{k}^{\rm RGO}  and  p_{k}^{\rm cap}  denote the mismatch
probabilities in the corresponding couplings, then 
 

 
  
 p_{k}^{\rm RGO}\leq a\,,\hskip 20.00003ptp_{k}^{\rm cap}\leq a\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathbb{E}N_{\nabla V}^{\rm RGO}\leq K_{\rm RGO}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt0\leq k<T\,. 
  
 
 
 
 For the proximal solver, let  x_{\star}  minimize  V . Under
 \pi_{\eta} , write
 Y=X+\sqrt{\eta}Z  with  X\sim\mu ,
 Z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I) , and  X\perp Z , and set 
 

 
  
 R(X,Y)\coloneqq\frac{\|(2X-Y)-\eta\nabla V(X)-X\|}{\sqrt{d\eta}}=\frac{\|\sqrt{\eta}Z+\eta\nabla V(X)\|}{\sqrt{d\eta}}\,. 
  
 (4.15) 
 
 Integration by parts gives
 \alpha\,\mathbb{E}\|X-x_{\star}\|^{2}\leq\mathbb{E}\langle\nabla V(X),X-x_{\star}\rangle=d .
Using  \|\nabla V(X)\|\leq\beta\,\|X-x_{\star}\| ,
 \beta\eta\leq 1 , ( D.7 ), and the Gaussian
norm tail, we obtain for  u\geq 1 
 
 
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{\pi_{\eta}}\bigl\{R(X,Y)>2\sqrt{\kappa}\,\bigl(1+\sqrt{2u/d}\bigr)\bigr\} 
  
 (4.16) 
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{P}\bigl\{\|X-x_{\star}\|>\sqrt{d/\alpha}+\sqrt{2u/\alpha}\bigr\}+\mathbb{P}\bigl\{\|Z\|>\sqrt{d}+\sqrt{2u}\bigr\}\leq 2e^{-u}\,. 
  
 
 Set  u_{0}\coloneqq\Delta+2\log(1/a)+\log 2 . After increasing  K ,
( 4.14 ) and the definition of
 Q_{\rm prox}  give 
 

 
  
 u_{0}\leq C\ell\,,\hskip 20.00003ptQ_{\rm prox}\geq K_{\rm prox}\,\bigl[1+\log\bigl\{1+2\sqrt{\kappa}\,\bigl(1+\sqrt{2u_{0}/d}\bigr)\bigr\}\bigr]\,. 
  
 
 If  \mathcal{F}  is the solver failure event, then
( 4.4 ) and
( 4.16 ) give, under stationarity, 
 

 
  
 \pi_{\eta}(\mathcal{F})\leq 2e^{-u_{0}}=e^{-\Delta}a^{2}\,. 
  
 
 Cauchy–Schwarz then yields 
 

 
  
 \nu_{k}^{+}(\mathcal{F})\leq e^{D_{2}(\nu_{k}^{+}\|\pi_{\eta})/2}\,\pi_{\eta}(\mathcal{F})^{1/2}\leq a\,. 
  
 
 
 
 Construct the preceding couplings along the ideal trajectory, using the
same Bernoulli branch variables in both chains. If  X_{T}^{\rm id} 
is the ideal output, a union bound gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{P}\{\widehat{X}\neq X_{T}^{\rm id}\}\leq\sum_{k=0}^{T-1}\bigl\{\nu_{k}^{+}(\mathcal{F})+p_{k}^{\rm RGO}+p_{k}^{\rm cap}\bigr\}\leq 3Ta=\frac{\varepsilon}{2}\,. 
  
 (4.17) 
 
 Consequently, 
 

 
  
 \operatorname{TV}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(\widehat{X}),\mu\bigr)\leq\mathbb{P}\{\widehat{X}\neq X_{T}^{\rm id}\}+\operatorname{TV}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(X_{T}^{\rm id}),\mu\bigr)\leq\varepsilon\,, 
  
 
 which proves ( 4.11 ). 
 
 
 For the query count, early termination can only help, and a half-turn
call uses one reference gradient query and an expected
 \pi\bar{\lambda}_{\rm impl}  queries at candidate times. Therefore 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}N_{\nabla V}\leq T\,\bigl[Q_{\rm prox}+K_{\rm RGO}+(1-\rho)\,(1+\pi\bar{\lambda}_{\rm impl})\bigr]\leq KT\,\bigl[Q_{\rm prox}+\beta\eta\,(\sqrt{d\ell}+\ell)\bigr]\,. 
  
 (4.18) 
 
 The last inequality uses  Q_{\rm prox}\geq 1 . Combining it with
( 4.13 ) proves
( 4.12 ).
∎ 
 
 
 
 Corollary 4.4   (Optimized gradient complexity) . 
 
 There exist universal constants  c_{\rm opt}\in(0,1)  and
 K_{\rm opt}\geq 1  such that the following holds. Suppose that
( 1.1 ) holds, fix  \Delta\geq 1  and
 \varepsilon\in(0,1/4) , and let  \mu_{0}  be a probability measure
on  \mathbb{R}^{d}  satisfying  D_{2}(\mu_{0}\|\mu)\leq\Delta . Set 
 

 
  
 L\coloneqq\Delta+\log\frac{K_{\rm opt}d\kappa}{\varepsilon}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\eta\coloneqq\frac{c_{\rm opt}}{\beta\,(\sqrt{dL}+L)}\,, 
  
 (4.19) 
 
 and run  Algorithm   2  from  X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}  using
( 4.8 )–( 4.9 ). Its output  \widehat{X}  satisfies 
 

 
  
 \operatorname{TV}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(\widehat{X}),\mu\bigr)\leq\varepsilon\,. 
  
 
 Moreover, if  N_{\nabla V}  is the total number of gradient queries,
then 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}N_{\nabla V}\leq K_{\rm opt}\sqrt{\kappa}\,(d+L)^{1/4}\,L^{3/4}\,\Bigl(\Delta+\log\frac{4}{\varepsilon}\Bigr)\log(\kappa L)\,. 
  
 (4.20) 
 
 In particular, when  \Delta=O(1) , the expected gradient
complexity is  \widetilde{O}(\sqrt{\kappa}\,d^{1/4}) , where
 \widetilde{O}  suppresses logarithmic factors in
 d ,  \kappa , and  1/\varepsilon . 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 We first verify that the tuned step size satisfies the hypotheses of
 Theorem   4.3 , and then simplify its query bound. Since
 d\geq 1 ,  \kappa\geq 1 , and  L\geq 1 , the choice in
( 4.19 ) satisfies  0<\eta\leq 1/\beta . Moreover, 
 

 
  
 \alpha\eta=\frac{c_{\rm opt}}{\kappa\,(\sqrt{dL}+L)}\,. 
  
 
 The definitions of  L  and  \ell  therefore imply, with
 K_{\rm opt}  large enough, that 
 

 
  
 \log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}\leq K_{\rm opt}L\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\ell\leq K_{\rm opt}L\,,\hskip 20.00003ptQ_{\rm prox}\leq K_{\rm opt}\log(\kappa L)\,. 
  
 (4.21) 
 
 The bound on  \ell  and the formula for  \eta  give 
 

 
  
 \beta\eta\,(\sqrt{d\ell}+\ell)\leq\frac{K_{\rm opt}c_{\rm opt}}{\sqrt{dL}+L}\,(\sqrt{dL}+L)=K_{\rm opt}c_{\rm opt}\,. 
  
 (4.22) 
 
 Choosing  c_{\rm opt}  small enough makes the last expression no larger
than the constant  c  in  Theorem   4.3 . This verifies
( 4.10 ), so the total variation conclusion
follows from  Theorem   4.3 . 
 
 
 It remains to simplify the query bound. The choice of  \eta  gives 
 

 
  
 \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}=\sqrt{\frac{\kappa}{c_{\rm opt}}}\,(\sqrt{dL}+L)^{1/2}\leq C\sqrt{\kappa}\,(dL+L^{2})^{1/4}\,. 
  
 (4.23) 
 
 Combining this estimate with the first inequality in
( 4.21 ) yields 
 

 
  
 \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\leq C\sqrt{\kappa}\,(dL+L^{2})^{1/4}\sqrt{L}\,. 
  
 
 The remaining bracket in ( 4.12 ) is, by
( 4.21 ) and ( 4.22 ), bounded by
a universal multiple of  \log(\kappa L) . Substitution into
( 4.12 ), with
 K_{\rm opt}  large enough, proves ( 4.20 ).
∎ 
 
 
  

