 (\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*})^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}(\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}})^{*}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{R}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{R}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}\,. 
  
 
 This proves self-adjointness; the Markov and contraction properties are
inherited from  \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}} .
∎ 

A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p7.1 Finally, Gaussian momentum averaging satisfies
 \mathsf{R}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}  and
 \mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{R}=\mathsf{\Pi}_{p} . Combining these identities with
( A.3 ) gives
A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p7.2 This proves self-adjointness; the Markov and contraction properties are
inherited from  \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{x}} .
∎
A1.SS2 
 A.2  The averaged ideal half-turn kernel 

 
 Proof of  Proposition   3.2 . 
 
 Fix  y . For each reference point  \widetilde{x} ,
 Proposition   A.1  shows that the fixed-reference
position operator
 \mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}  is a self-adjoint
Markov contraction on  L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}) . The reference
point in  Algorithm   1  is drawn independently from
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} . Consequently, the conditional half-turn
operator is the average 
 

 
  
 (\mathsf{H}_{y}f)(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\bigl(\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{x}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{p}^{*}f\bigr)(x)\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}\widetilde{x})\,. 
  
 
 The averaging measure does not depend on  x , so  \mathsf{H}_{y}  is again a
self-adjoint Markov contraction. In kernel form, this is the
detailed-balance identity 
 

 
  
 \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})=\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(x^{\prime},\mathrm{d}x)\,, 
  
 
 which proves the proposition.
∎ 
 
 
 
 The preceding reversibility statement holds for each fixed  y . To use
these kernels as a single operator on the augmented space, we next verify
their joint measurability and then integrate the conditional operator
identities over  y . 
 
 
 Proposition A.2   (Joint measurability and augmented space half-turn operator) . 
 
 The family of conditional half-turn kernels
 (\mathcal{H}_{y})_{y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}  admits a version, again denoted by
 \mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}) , such that
 (y,x)\mapsto\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,A)  is Borel measurable for every
Borel set  A\subset\mathbb{R}^{d} . Consequently, 
 

 
  
 (\mathsf{H}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x^{\prime},y)\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}) 
  
 (A.9) 
 
 defines a self-adjoint Markov contraction on  L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) . 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 Since  \nabla V  is continuous, the harmonic flow, event rate, and
reflection map are jointly Borel; the convention  R_{0}=I  ensures
this even where the reflection normal vanishes. The integrated hazard
is jointly Borel, with continuous, nondecreasing dependence on time, so its
threshold-crossing times are Borel, as can be checked using rational
times. Thus the recursion
( A.1 )–( A.2 )
makes each jump time and post-jump state Borel in
 (y,x_{0},p_{0},\widetilde{x},(E_{j})_{j\geq 1}) .
By non-explosion, the terminal state at time  \pi  is the almost-sure
limit of the Borel terminal states obtained by allowing at most  n 
jumps. Setting the limit to zero wherever it does not exist gives a
Borel version. 
 
 
 The conditional density in ( 2.8 ) is jointly
Borel in  (y,\widetilde{x}) . Integrating  \mathbf{1}_{\{x_{\pi}\in A\}} 
against this reference law and the laws of the Gaussian momentum and
exponential clocks therefore makes  (y,x)\mapsto\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,A) 
Borel for every Borel set  A . 
 
 
 Finally, each  \mathsf{H}_{y}  is a self-adjoint Markov contraction by
 Proposition   3.2 . Integrating the conditional contraction
bound gives 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|\mathsf{H}f\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})}^{2} 
 \displaystyle\leq\int\bigl\|f(\cdot,y)\bigr\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y})}^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)=\|f\|_{L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})}^{2}\,. 
  
 
 Conditional self-adjointness and Fubini likewise give
 \langle f,\mathsf{H}g\rangle=\langle\mathsf{H}f,g\rangle  for bounded
measurable  f,g , and hence for all  L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})  by density.
Positivity and  \mathsf{H}1=1  follow from the corresponding properties of
each  \mathsf{H}_{y} .
∎ 
 
 

