A2.SS3.p11.1 Combining
 Lemmas   B.3  and  B.4 , we can now
prove the  L^{2}  contraction in  Theorem   3.5 .
A2.SS3.4 
 Proof of  Theorem   3.5 . 
 
 We first turn the one-step Lyapunov decay into a fixed  L^{2}  contraction
over a block of  N  steps, and then apply this contraction to densities. 
 
 
 Take  c_{0}  from  Lemma   B.4  and choose a fixed
universal  \rho_{\star}\in(0,1/2] . The one-step estimate requires
 \rho\leq 1/2 . Since
 \alpha\eta\leq\beta\eta\leq 1  and
 x\log(e/x)\leq 1  for  0<x\leq 1 , the prescribed refreshment
probability satisfies 
 

 
  
 0<\rho=\rho_{\star}\sqrt{\alpha\eta\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\leq\rho_{\star}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,. 
  
 
 
 
 The decay rate in  Lemma   B.4  is
 \rho/\Lambda_{\rho} , so a fixed contraction is obtained by taking
 N  proportional to  \Lambda_{\rho}/\rho . We first bound this ratio.
Recall that
 \gamma_{\rm gap}\asymp\sqrt{\alpha\eta}  and
 \log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\asymp\log(e/(\alpha\eta)) . Hence 
 

 
  
 \Bigl(\frac{\rho}{\gamma_{\rm gap}}\Bigr)^{2}\leq C\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\rho\geq c\gamma_{\rm gap}\sqrt{\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})}\,. 
  
 
 It follows from the definition of  \Lambda_{\rho}  that 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\frac{\Lambda_{\rho}}{\rho} 
 \displaystyle\leq C\,\frac{\sqrt{\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})}}{\gamma_{\rm gap}}\leq\frac{C}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,. 
  
 
 Choose a sufficiently large universal constant  C_{1}  and consider 
 

 
  
 N\geq C_{1}\,\frac{\Lambda_{\rho}}{\rho}\,. 
  
 (B.32) 
 
 For  f\in L_{0}^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) , take  \omega  as in
( B.25 ). Iterating
( B.26 ) over this block gives 
 

 
  
 \mathscr{L}_{\omega}(\mathsf{K}^{N}f)\leq\Bigl(1-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)^{N}\,\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\,. 
  
 
 Applying the lower bound in ( B.22 ) to
 \mathsf{K}^{N}f  and the upper bound to  f , we obtain 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|\mathsf{K}^{N}f\|^{2} 
 \displaystyle\leq 2\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(\mathsf{K}^{N}f)\leq 2\,\Bigl(1-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)^{N}\,\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\leq 3\,\Bigl(1-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)^{N}\,\|f\|^{2}\leq 3e^{-cC_{1}}\,\|f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 
 The last step uses  1-x\leq e^{-x}  and
 N\geq C_{1}\Lambda_{\rho}/\rho .
Taking  C_{1}  sufficiently large makes  3e^{-cC_{1}}\leq e^{-1} . Thus, 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{K}^{N}f\|^{2}\leq e^{-1}\,\|f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (B.33) 
 
 Iterating by blocks, the  L^{2}  contraction
( B.33 ) is equivalently, by ( D.5 ) and the
adjoint remark following it, the  \chi^{2} -estimate 
 

 
  
 \chi^{2}\bigl(\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN}\|\pi_{\eta}\bigr)\leq e^{-J}\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\,, 
  
 
 which proves ( 3.14 ). The  X -marginal of
 \pi_{\eta}  is  \mu , and total variation cannot increase under
projection. Moreover, Cauchy–Schwarz gives
 \operatorname{TV}(P,Q)\leq\tfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{\chi^{2}(P\|Q)} , which gives ( 3.15 ).
∎ 
 

