 Appendix C  Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound 

 
 This appendix establishes the two model-specific estimates used by the
modified  L^{2}  method argument of Appendix  B : the
macroscopic coercivity lemma
( Lemma   B.1 ) and the global half-turn bound
( Proposition   B.2 ). 
 
 
 C.1  Proof of
 Lemma   B.1 

 
 Proof of  Lemma   B.1 . 
 
 We first prove
( B.13 ). It suffices to consider
 f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d}) , the general case following by density and
closedness of the gradient. 
 
 
 Recall that the conditional
density  p_{y}  of  Y_{-}  given  Y_{+}=y  is 
 

 
  
 p_{y}(u)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V((y+u)/2)-\frac{\|y-u\|^{2}}{8\eta}\Bigr\}\,, 
  
 
 and define 
 

 
  
 s_{y}(u)\coloneqq-\frac{1}{2}\,\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\,. 
  
 
 Differentiating the normalized conditional density gives 
 

 
  
 \nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}\bigl(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\,. 
  
 (C.1) 
 
 
 
 The negative log-density of  p_{y}  has Hessian 
 

 
  
 \frac{1}{4}\,\bigl\{\nabla^{2}V((y+u)/2)+\eta^{-1}I\bigr\}\succeq\frac{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}{4}\,I\,. 
  
 
 Hence, the conditional Poincaré inequality implies, for every unit
vector  a , 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr) 
 \displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|\nabla_{u}(a^{\top}s_{y})(Y_{-})\|^{2}\,\bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\bigr]\,. 
  
 
 The assumptions
 \alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V\preceq\beta I  and
 \beta\eta\leq 1  give 
 

 
  
 \|\nabla_{u}s_{y}(u)\|_{\rm op}\leq\frac{\eta^{-1}-\alpha}{4}\,. 
  
 
 Consequently, 
 

 
  
 \Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\,. 
  
 
 Applying conditional Cauchy–Schwarz in
( C.1 ) and taking the supremum over
 \|a\|=1  yields 
 

 
  
 \|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)\|^{2}\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 Integrating in  y  and using ( B.9 ), 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{(1-\alpha\eta)^{2}}{4\,(1+\alpha\eta)}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.2) 
 
 Finally,
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|f\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2} ,
which proves ( B.13 ). 
 
 
 We next bound the spectrum of  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} . Let  f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} .
Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  preserves constants,  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f  is centered.
The Poincaré inequality for the marginal law  \pi_{\eta}^{Y}  gives 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{Y}}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)\leq\Bigl(\frac{1}{\alpha}+\eta\Bigr)\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.3) 
 
 Combining ( C.3 ) with
( C.2 ) and rearranging gives 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|\leq\frac{1-\alpha\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\|f\|=\rho_{\rm mac}\,\|f\|\,. 
  
 (C.4) 
 
 Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  is self-adjoint, this proves 
 

 
  
 -\rho_{\rm mac}I\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\preceq\rho_{\rm mac}I\,. 
  
 
 It remains only to obtain the uniform lower bound
 \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\succeq-\frac{1}{2}\,I .
For every  g\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) , exchangeability and
( B.8 ) give 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle 
 \displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\bigl(g(Y_{+})-g(Y_{-})\bigr)^{2}\bigr]=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(X+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(X-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr]\,. 
  
 (C.5) 
 
 For fixed  X=x , the Gaussian Poincaré inequality implies 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr] 
 \displaystyle\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl\|\nabla g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)+\nabla g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr\|^{2}\,. 
  
 
 Averaging in  X  and using that  Y_{+}  and  Y_{-}  have the same
marginal gives 
 

 
  
 \bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla g\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.6) 
 
 
 
 Applying ( C.6 ) with  g=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f  and using
( B.13 ), we obtain 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\bigl\langle f,\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})f\bigr\rangle 
 \displaystyle=\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl\langle f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})f\bigr\rangle\,. 
  
 
 Thus, as an operator inequality,  2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\preceq I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2} ,
or equivalently 
 

 
  
 (I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})^{2}(I+2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\succeq 0\,. 
  
 (C.7) 
 
 Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  is a self-adjoint contraction, its spectrum lies in
 [-1,1] . The scalar inequality corresponding to
( C.7 ) is 
 

 
  
 (1-\lambda)^{2}(1+2\lambda)\geq 0\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\lambda\in\operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\,, 
  
 
 and therefore  \operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\subseteq[-1/2,1] . Hence
 -\frac{1}{2}I\,\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} .
Together with ( C.4 ), this proves
( B.14 ). 
 
 
 Finally, 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\succeq(1-\rho_{\rm mac}^{2})\,I=\frac{4\alpha\eta}{(1+\alpha\eta)^{2}}\,I\,, 
  
 
 and therefore 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\frac{2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}{1+\alpha\eta}\,I=\gamma_{\rm gap}I\,. 
  
 
 This proves ( B.15 ).
∎ 
 
 
 
 The half-turn analysis below uses the following alternative form of
( C.1 ). 
 
 
 Lemma C.1   (Derivative formula for  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} ) . 
 
 For every  f\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})  and  \pi_{\eta}^{Y} -almost every  y , 
 

 
  
 \nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 (C.8) 
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 First let  f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d}) . The representation
( C.1 ) gives 
 

 
  
 \nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 Using the definition of  s_{y} , we write 
 

 
  
 s_{y}(u)=\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\Bigr\}-\nabla V((y+u)/2)\,. 
  
 
 To rewrite ( C.1 ) in the claimed form, we
only need to identify the covariance generated by the vector in braces.
Integration by parts in  u  against the conditional density  p_{y}  gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\ \Bigm|\ Y_{+}=y\Bigr]\,. 
  
 (C.9) 
 
 Applying ( C.9 ) to  f\equiv 1 , justified by compactly
supported cutoffs converging to  1 , gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}\Bigl[\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]=0\,. 
  
 
 Combining this with ( C.9 ), we obtain 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y) 
 \displaystyle=\mathrm{Cov}\Bigl(f(Y_{-}),\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr)-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y) 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y) 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 This is ( C.8 ) for smooth  f . 
 
 
 The extension to  H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})  is a standard approximation
argument once we check that the covariance term is continuous in
 L^{2} . By ( 2.11 ), the conditional
law of  X  given  Y_{+}=y  has Poincaré constant at most
 \eta/(1+\alpha\eta)\leq\eta . Hence, for every unit vector
 a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , 
 

 
  
 \Var(a^{\top}\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla^{2}V(X)\,a\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\leq\beta^{2}\eta\,. 
  
 
 Conditional Cauchy–Schwarz and
( B.9 ) therefore imply 
 

 
  
 \bigl\|\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+})\bigr\|_{L^{2}}\leq\beta\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|\,, 
  
 
 and the same bound applies to differences. Conditional expectation is
an  L^{2}  contraction, and ( B.13 ) controls the
left side. Thus every term in ( C.8 ) is
continuous under  H^{1}  approximation. Density of
 C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})  in  H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})  completes the proof.
∎ 
 
 
 
 
 C.2  The half-turn operator on affine functions 

 
 This subsection establishes a conditional half-turn estimate for affine
observables. After rescaling  X-y  by the Gaussian scale
 \sqrt{\eta} , we show that the half-turn nearly maps every affine
function with mean zero to its negative. The proof combines a Duhamel
representation of the half-turn with covariance identities for the
rescaled conditional law. 
 
 
 In  Section   C.3 , this estimate will be applied to
the conditional affine part of  \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w  in the proof of
 Proposition   B.2 . 
 
 
 We first fix  y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}  and work under the conditional law of  X 
given  Y=y . The bounds below are uniform in  y  and will later be
integrated over  y . To put the Gaussian confinement and harmonic
dynamics on unit scale, set 
 

 
  
 W\coloneqq\frac{X-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptq_{y}\coloneqq\Law(W\mid Y=y)\,. 
  
 
 The density of  q_{y}  and the scaled potential gradient are 
 

 
  
 q_{y}(\mathrm{d}w)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(y+\sqrt{\eta}w)-\frac{1}{2}\|w\|^{2}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}w\,,\hskip 20.00003ptg_{y}(w)\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V(y+\sqrt{\eta}w)\,. 
  
 
 Thus, the negative log-density of  q_{y}  has Hessian
 I+\nabla g_{y} , and 
 

 
  
 \nabla\log q_{y}(w)=-(w+g_{y}(w))\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\alpha\eta I\preceq\nabla g_{y}(w)\preceq\beta\eta I\,. 
  
 (C.10) 
 
 In particular,  q_{y}  is  1 -strongly log-concave. We use the same
symbol  \mathsf{H}_{y}  for the conditional half-turn operator in the
normalized coordinate  w=(x-y)/\sqrt{\eta} : 
 

 
  
 (\mathsf{H}_{y}f)(w)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f\Bigl(\frac{x^{\prime}-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}\Bigr)\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(y+\sqrt{\eta}w,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\,. 
  
 
 By  Proposition   3.2 ,  \mathsf{H}_{y}  is a self-adjoint Markov
contraction on  L^{2}(q_{y}) . 
 
 
 Put 
 

 
  
 \bar{w}_{y}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}W\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\Sigma_{y}\coloneqq\mathrm{Cov}_{q_{y}}(W)\,. 
  
 
 Thus,  \Sigma_{y}  is the conditional covariance in the normalized
coordinate; equivalently, we have the identity
 \mathrm{Cov}(X\mid Y=y)=\eta\Sigma_{y} . Accordingly, 
 

 
  
 I-\Sigma_{y}=\eta\nabla^{2}U_{\eta}(y)\,, 
  
 
 by ( D.10 ). Thus  I-\Sigma_{y}  is both the covariance
defect relative to a standard Gaussian and the normalized curvature of
the  Y -marginal. For  a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , write
 \ell_{a,y}:w\mapsto a^{\top}(w-\bar{w}_{y}) ; this affine function has mean zero
under  q_{y} . 
 
 
 The main result of this subsection is the following estimate. 
 
 
 Proposition C.2   (Weighted half-turn estimate for affine functions) . 
 
 Assume  \beta\eta\leq 1/2 . Uniformly in  y , every
 \ell_{a,y}:w\mapsto a^{\top}(w-\bar{w}_{y})  satisfies 
 

 
  
 \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{y})\ell_{a,y}\|_{L^{2}(q_{y})}^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\,a^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a\,. 
  
 (C.11) 
 
 More generally, suppose that  y\mapsto a_{y}  is measurable and
 \int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)<\infty , and define 
 

 
  
 \ell(x,y)\coloneqq a_{y}^{\top}\Bigl(\frac{x-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}-\bar{w}_{y}\Bigr)\,. 
  
 
 Then,  \ell\in\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}  and 
 

 
  
 \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\ell\|^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\,. 
  
 
 
 
 
 We prove the proposition after two preparatory results. The first gives a
Duhamel representation of the deviation from exact sign reversal; the
second bounds the resulting term in terms of  I-\Sigma_{y} . 
 
 
 To analyze  \mathsf{H}_{y}\ell_{a,y} , first fix a reference point
 \widetilde{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}  in the rescaled coordinate. The resulting
phase-space process is 
 

 
  
 \begin{gathered}\dot{w}=p\,,\qquad\dot{p}=-\{w+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}\,,\\
\text{event rate }[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}\,,\\
p\longmapsto R_{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})}p\quad\text{at a bounce}\,.\end{gathered} 
  
 
 The construction and path reversal arguments of
Appendix  A  apply verbatim, with phase space
law  q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I) . Denote the resulting strongly continuous
contraction semigroup by
 (\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}})_{t\geq 0} . On smooth compactly
supported observables, its generator is 
 

 
  
 \begin{split}\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}f(w,p)&\coloneqq p^{\top}\nabla_{w}f(w,p)-\{w+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}^{\top}\nabla_{p}f(w,p)\\
&\qquad{}+[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}\bigl\{f(w,R_{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})}p)-f(w,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split} 
  
 
 The operator  \mathsf{H}_{y}  is
recovered by drawing  \widetilde{W}\sim q_{y} , running the corresponding
process for time  \pi , and averaging over  \widetilde{W}  and the
initial Gaussian momentum. 
 
 
 If  g_{y}\equiv 0 , then  q_{y}=\mathcal{N}(0,I) ,  \bar{w}_{y}=0 , and the bounce
rate vanishes. The dynamics reduce to harmonic motion, 
 

 
  
 w_{t}=w_{0}\cos t+p_{0}\sin t\,,\hskip 20.00003ptp_{t}=-w_{0}\sin t+p_{0}\cos t\,, 
  
 
 so  \ell_{a,y}(w_{\pi})=-\ell_{a,y}(w_{0}) . For general  g_{y} , the
discrepancy between the harmonic center  -g_{y}(\widetilde{w})  and the
conditional mean  \bar{w}_{y} , together with the residual gradient
bounces, produces an inhomogeneous term. The next lemma identifies this
term. 
 
 
 Lemma C.3   (Affine Duhamel formula) . 
 
 For  a\in\mathbb{R}^{d}  and  \widetilde{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , set 
 

 
  
 k_{a,\widetilde{w}}(w,p)\coloneqq-a^{\top}\{\bar{w}_{y}+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}-2\,[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}^{2}\,\frac{a^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}}{\|g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\|^{2}}\,, 
  
 
 where the quotient is defined as zero when
 g_{y}(w)=g_{y}(\widetilde{w}) . Then
 k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\in L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) , and for every
 t\geq 0 , 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y}=\ell_{a,y}\cos t+a^{\top}p\sin t+\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\sin(t-s)\,\mathrm{d}s 
  
 
 in  L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) . In particular, 
 

 
  
 (I+\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{w}})\ell_{a,y}=\int_{0}^{\pi}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\sin s\,\mathrm{d}s\,. 
  
 (C.12) 
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 The generator maps the position observable  \ell_{a,y}  to the
momentum observable  a^{\top}p , and maps  a^{\top}p  back to
 -\ell_{a,y}+k_{a,\widetilde{w}} . Thus, this pair evolves as a harmonic
system with the additional term  k_{a,\widetilde{w}} , and variation of
constants gives the claimed formula. We first establish the required
 L^{2}  bounds and justify these generator identities for the unbounded
observables. 
 
 
 First,  k_{a,\widetilde{w}}  is square integrable. Its first term is
constant in  (w,p) , while the absolute value of its second term is at
most 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle 2\,[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}^{2}\,\frac{|a^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}|}{\|g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\|^{2}} 
 \displaystyle\leq 2\,\|a\|\,\|p\|^{2}\,\|g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\| 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\leq 2\beta\eta\,\|a\|\,\|p\|^{2}\,\|w-\widetilde{w}\|\,. 
  
 
 Since  q_{y}  is  1 -strongly log-concave and the momentum is Gaussian,
this bound shows that  k_{a,\widetilde{w}}  belongs to
 L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) . The same moment bounds give
 \ell_{a,y},a^{\top}p\in L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) . 
 
 
 Using
 a^{\top}(R_{h}p-p)=-2(p^{\top}h)(a^{\top}h)/\|h\|^{2}  and
 [u]_{+}u=[u]_{+}^{2} , the generator formula gives 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y} 
 \displaystyle=a^{\top}p\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}(a^{\top}p)=-\ell_{a,y}+k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\,. 
  
 
 To justify these identities for the unbounded observables, set  H_{\widetilde{w}}(w,p)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\,\{\|w+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\|^{2}+\|p\|^{2}\} ,
which is preserved by both the flow and the bounces. Let  \chi_{R}  be
a smooth cutoff equal to one on  [0,R]  and zero on
 [2R,\infty) . Conservation of  H_{\widetilde{w}}  gives, for
 F\in\{\ell_{a,y},a^{\top}p\} ,
 \mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}\{\chi_{R}(H_{\widetilde{w}})F\}=\chi_{R}(H_{\widetilde{w}})\,\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}F .
Applying Dynkin’s formula and then letting  R\to\infty  in  L^{2} ,
using the preceding integrability bounds and semigroup contractivity,
yields 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y}-\ell_{a,y} 
 \displaystyle=\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}(a^{\top}p)\,\mathrm{d}s\,, 
  
 
  
 \displaystyle\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}(a^{\top}p)-a^{\top}p 
 \displaystyle=\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}(-\ell_{a,y}+k_{a,\widetilde{w}})\,\mathrm{d}s\,. 
  
 
 Applying variation of constants to this integral system gives 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y}=\ell_{a,y}\cos t+a^{\top}p\sin t+\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\sin(t-s)\,\mathrm{d}s\,. 
  
 
 This is the first identity in the lemma. At  t=\pi , the homogeneous
part is  -\ell_{a,y}  and  \sin(\pi-s)=\sin s , which gives
( C.12 ).
∎ 
 
 
 
 The proof of  Proposition   C.2  will also use the
following identities and bounds. 
 
 
 Lemma C.4   (Conditional gradient covariance bound) . 
 
 Fix  y  and assume  \beta\eta\leq 1/2 . Then
 \mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}g_{y}(W)=-\bar{w}_{y} , and 
 

 
  
 \mathrm{Cov}_{q_{y}}(W,g_{y}(W))=I-\Sigma_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathrm{Cov}_{q_{y}}(g_{y}(W))\preceq\frac{\beta\eta}{1-\beta\eta}\,(I-\Sigma_{y})\preceq 2\beta\eta\,(I-\Sigma_{y})\,. 
  
 (C.13) 
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 Under  Y_{+}=y , we have  X=y+\sqrt{\eta}W  and
 Y_{-}=y+2\sqrt{\eta}W . Applying ( C.9 ) to
 f:u\mapsto F((u-y)/(2\sqrt{\eta}))  therefore gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}\nabla F(W)=\mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}[F(W)\,\{W+g_{y}(W)\}]\,. 
  
 
 Taking  F\equiv 1  and then the coordinate functions of  W  and
 g_{y}(W) , with the same cutoff approximation as in
 Lemma   C.1 , gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}g_{y}(W)=-\bar{w}_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathrm{Cov}(W,g_{y}(W))=I-\Sigma_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathbb{E}\nabla g_{y}(W)=I-\Sigma_{y}+\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))\,. 
  
 
 For every  a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , the same conditional Poincaré estimate used in
the proof of  Lemma   C.1 , together with
 (\nabla g_{y})^{2}\preceq\beta\eta\,\nabla g_{y}  then give 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle a^{\top}\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))a 
 \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{E}\|\nabla g_{y}(W)\,a\|^{2}\leq\beta\eta\,a^{\top}\{I-\Sigma_{y}+\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))\}a\,. 
  
 
 Rearranging proves ( C.13 ).
∎ 
 
 
 
 Proof of  Proposition   C.2 . 
 
 Fix  y , and let
 \widetilde{W}\sim q_{y}  be independent of
 (W,P)\sim q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I) . We first apply the Duhamel formula to
identify the estimate that remains to be proved. Using, in order,
Jensen’s inequality in  \widetilde{W} ,
( C.12 ), the Bochner triangle inequality,
the contractivity of Gaussian momentum averaging and
 \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}} , and
 \int_{0}^{\pi}\sin t\,\mathrm{d}t=2 , we obtain 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{y})\ell_{a,y}\|_{L^{2}(q_{y})}^{2} 
 \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{W}}\bigl\|\mathbb{E}_{P}[(I+\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{W}})\ell_{a,y}]\bigr\|_{L^{2}(q_{y})}^{2} 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{W}}\Bigl(\int_{0}^{\pi}\|\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{W}}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}\|_{L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I))}\sin t\,\mathrm{d}t\Bigr)^{2}\leq 4\,\mathbb{E}_{W,\widetilde{W},P}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}^{2}\,. 
  
 
 It remains to bound the second moment on the right. 
 
 
 Conditional on  (W,\widetilde{W}) , a direct calculation with the
Gaussian momentum  P , together with
 \mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}g_{y}(W)=-\bar{w}_{y}  from
 Lemma   C.4 , gives 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{P}[k_{a,\widetilde{W}}\mid W,\widetilde{W}] 
 \displaystyle=-a^{\top}\{g_{y}(W)-\mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}g_{y}(W)\}\,, 
  
 
  
 \displaystyle\Var_{P}(k_{a,\widetilde{W}}\mid W,\widetilde{W}) 
 \displaystyle=5\,[a^{\top}\{g_{y}(W)-g_{y}(\widetilde{W})\}]^{2}\,. 
  
 
 After averaging over the independent variables  W  and
 \widetilde{W} , the conditional variance term contributes
 10\Var_{q_{y}}(a^{\top}g_{y}(W)) , while the squared conditional mean
contributes one more copy of the same variance. Consequently, 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}_{W,\widetilde{W},P}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}^{2}=11\Var_{q_{y}}(a^{\top}g_{y}(W))\,. 
  
 
 By  Lemma   C.4 , 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}_{W,\widetilde{W},P}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\,a^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a\,. 
  
 
 Combining the last estimate with the Duhamel bound proves
( C.11 ). For the global
assertion,  Proposition   2.1  gives
 I-\Sigma_{y}\succeq\alpha\eta\,(1+\alpha\eta)^{-1}\,I , so the stated
integrability condition implies  \ell\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) . Its
conditional mean is zero. Applying the conditional inequality with
 a=a_{y}  and integrating in  y  proves the result.
∎ 
 
 
 
 
 C.3  Proof of
 Proposition   B.2 :
From affine estimates to the half-turn bound 

 
 The following proposition controls the part of the  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} -spectrum near
 +1 . 
 
 
 Proposition C.5   (Interpolation bound on the  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} -spectral subspace
associated with  [\frac{1}{2},1] ) . 
 
 Assume  \beta\eta\leq c . If
 w\in\operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[1/2,1]}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})) , then 
 

 
  
 \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w\|^{2}\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}w\|\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{3}w\|\,. 
  
 (C.14) 
 
 
 
 
 We now prove  Proposition   B.2 . 
 
 
 Proof of  Proposition   B.2 . 
 
 We regard  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  and  \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}  as operators on
 \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}  and work with the spectral subspaces 
 

 
  
 \mathcal{H}\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[1/2,1]}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}))\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathcal{H}^{\prime}\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[-1/2,1/2)}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}))\,. 
  
 
 Recall that
 \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1} , and therefore
 Proposition   C.5  yields 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2} 
 \displaystyle=\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}v\|^{2}\leq C\,\|v\|\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}v\|\,\hskip 20.00003pt\forall v\in\mathcal{H}\,. 
  
 
 Recall that by  Lemma   B.1 ,
 \gamma_{\rm gap}^{2}I\preceq\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\preceq I .
Applying  Lemma   D.4  with  \mathsf{A}=C^{-1/2}\,(\mathsf{T}^{*}\mathsf{T})^{1/2} ,  \mathsf{B}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}|_{\mathcal{H}} ,
 m=\gamma_{\rm gap}^{2} , and  M=1 , where  \mathsf{T}\coloneqq(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}|_{\mathcal{H}} , gives a factor
 1+\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap}) , up to a universal constant. Shrinking the
universal constant  c  in the assumption if necessary, we may suppose that
 \beta\eta\leq 1/16 . Then
 \gamma_{\rm gap}\leq 2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}\leq 1/2 , so the additive constant is
absorbed by  \log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap}) . Therefore, 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2}\leq C\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2}\,\hskip 20.00003pt\forall v\in\mathcal{H}\,. 
  
 
 On the other hand, on  \mathcal{H}^{\prime} , no further spectral localization
is needed. Since  \mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}  is a contraction,
 \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}  is an isometry, and
 \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\succeq\frac{3}{4}I  on  \mathcal{H}^{\prime} , we have 
 

 
  
 \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\|\leq 2\,\|v\|\leq 4\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}v\|\,\hskip 20.00003pt\forall v\in\mathcal{H}^{\prime}\,. 
  
 
 For a general  v\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} , decompose
 v=v_{\mathcal{H}}+v_{\mathcal{H}^{\prime}} . Combining the preceding two
estimates with  (a+b)^{2}\leq 2a^{2}+2b^{2} , and using that  \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}} 
commutes with  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} , proves
( B.17 ).
∎ 
 
 
 
 We prove  Proposition   C.5  after the following preparatory results. The first
approximates  \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w , conditionally on  Y_{+} , by a function
that is affine in the rescaled coordinate  W=(X-Y_{+})/\sqrt{\eta} , so
that  Proposition   C.2  applies. The second bounds the resulting
affine data by the gradients of  w  and  (I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w , using the
derivative formula ( C.8 ). Both results hold
for arbitrary functions in  H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) ; the spectral
condition enters when we invert  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} . 
 
 
 Recall from  Section   C.2  the conditional law
 q_{y}\coloneqq\Law(W\mid Y_{+}=y) , its mean  \bar{w}_{y}  and covariance
 \Sigma_{y} , and that, conditionally on  Y_{+}=y , we have
 X=y+\sqrt{\eta}\,W  and  Y_{-}=y+2\sqrt{\eta}\,W . 
 
 
 Lemma C.6   (Conditional affine approximation and energy bound) . 
 
 Let  w\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) , and let
 a_{y}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}[\nabla w(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]  denote the conditional mean of
the gradient. For  \pi_{\eta}^{Y} -almost every  y , define 
 

 
  
 e_{y}(u)\coloneqq w(y+2\sqrt{\eta}u)-(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}w)(y)-2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y}^{\top}(u-\bar{w}_{y})\,,\hskip 20.00003ptu\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\,. 
  
 
 Then,  e_{y}  has  q_{y} -mean zero, the decomposition 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w=2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{Y_{+}}^{\top}(W-\bar{w}_{Y_{+}})+e_{Y_{+}}(W) 
  
 (C.15) 
 
 holds in  L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) , and, with 
 

 
  
 \mathcal{R}\coloneqq\|\nabla w\|^{2}-\int\|a_{y}\|^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)=\mathbb{E}\|\nabla w(Y_{-})-a_{Y_{+}}\|^{2}\,, 
  
 (C.16) 
 
 one has 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}[e_{Y_{+}}(W)^{2}]\leq 4\eta\mathcal{R}\,. 
  
 (C.17) 
 
 Moreover, if  \beta\eta\leq c  for a sufficiently small
universal constant  c>0 , then 
 

 
  
 \mathcal{R}+\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq C\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\|\,. 
  
 (C.18) 
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 By definition, 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w=w(Y_{-})-\mathbb{E}[w(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}]=w(Y_{-})-(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}w)(Y_{+})\,. 
  
 
 Conditioning on  Y_{+}=y  and substituting  Y_{-}=y+2\sqrt{\eta}\,W 
gives, for  \pi_{\eta}^{Y} -almost every  y , 
 

 
  
 (\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w)(u)=w(y+2\sqrt{\eta}u)-(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}w)(y)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptu\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\,, 
  
 
 which is ( C.15 ). This function
belongs to  L^{2}(q_{y})  and has  q_{y} -mean zero; since the
subtracted affine function is  q_{y} -centered,  e_{y}  has
 q_{y} -mean zero as well. 
 
 
 The coefficient  2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y}  is chosen to center the gradient
of  e_{y} : differentiating the preceding identity and averaging
under  q_{y}  gives 
 

 
  
 \int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\nabla_{u}(\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w)(u)\,q_{y}(\mathrm{d}u)=2\sqrt{\eta}\,\mathbb{E}[\nabla w(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]=2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y}\,, 
  
 
 and hence 
 

 
  
 \nabla_{u}e_{y}(W)=2\sqrt{\eta}\,\{\nabla w(Y_{-})-a_{y}\}\,. 
  
 
 Since  q_{y}  has Poincaré constant at most  1 , it follows that 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}[e_{y}(W)^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\leq\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla_{u}e_{y}(W)\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]=4\eta\,\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla w(Y_{-})-a_{y}\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\,. 
  
 
 Integrating in  y  and expanding the square using the definition of
 a_{y}  proves ( C.17 ). 
 
 
 We turn to the energy estimate. We may assume  \beta\eta\leq 1/2 .
Recall that
 g_{y}(u)=\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V(y+\sqrt{\eta}u) , so that
 \nabla V(X)=\eta^{-1/2}g_{y}(W) , and that
 \mathrm{Cov}(W,g_{y}(W)\mid Y_{+}=y)=I-\Sigma_{y}  by
 Lemma   C.4 . The decomposition
( C.15 ) therefore gives 
 

 
  
 \mathrm{Cov}(w(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)=2(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}+\eta^{-1/2}\varepsilon_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\varepsilon_{y}\coloneqq\mathrm{Cov}(e_{y}(W),g_{y}(W)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 Substituting this into ( C.8 ) yields 
 

 
  
 [\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w](y)=\nabla w(y)-a_{y}+2(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}+\eta^{-1/2}\varepsilon_{y}\,. 
  
 (C.19) 
 
 Conditional Cauchy–Schwarz and
 Lemma   C.4  give, for every
 v\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , 
 

 
  
 |\varepsilon_{y}^{\top}v|^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\,\mathbb{E}[e_{y}(W)^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\,v^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})v\,. 
  
 (C.20) 
 
 We will also use 
 

 
  
 0\preceq I-\Sigma_{y}\preceq\beta\eta I\,. 
  
 (C.21) 
 
 The two-sided bound in
 Lemma   B.1 , together with the fact that
 \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  fixes constants, gives 
 

 
  
 (I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})^{2}\preceq 3\,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}) 
  
 
 on  L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) . Since
 a_{y}=(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\nabla w)(y) , with  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  acting coordinatewise, 
 

 
  
 \mathcal{R}=\bigl\langle\nabla w,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})\nabla w\bigr\rangle\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\int\|\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\|^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq 3\mathcal{R}\,. 
  
 (C.22) 
 
 
 
 We now control  \mathcal{R}  and the affine energy simultaneously.
Take the  L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})  inner product of
( C.19 ) with the vector field
 y\mapsto\nabla w(y)+a_{y} . Since 
 

 
  
 \int\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)=\mathcal{R}\,, 
  
 
 we obtain the exact identity 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathcal{R}+4\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) 
 \displaystyle=\int[\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w](y)^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt-2\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt-\eta^{-1/2}\int\varepsilon_{y}^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\,. 
  
 (C.23) 
 
 
 
 We bound the three terms on the right separately. For the first,
conditional Jensen gives  \int\|a_{y}\|^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq\|\nabla w\|^{2} ,
and hence 
 

 
  
 \Bigl\lvert\int[\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w](y)^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\rvert\leq 2\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\|\,. 
  
 (C.24) 
 
 For the second, Cauchy–Schwarz,
( C.21 ), and
( C.22 ) give 
 

 
  
  
 \displaystyle 2\,\Bigl|\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr|\leq C\sqrt{\beta\eta}\,\mathcal{R}^{1/2}\,\Bigl\{\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2}\,. 
  
 (C.25) 
 
 Finally, using the pointwise identity
 \nabla w(y)+a_{y}=\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}+2a_{y} ,
( C.21 ) and
( C.22 ) imply 
 

 
  
  
 \displaystyle\int\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq C\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}+8\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\,. 
  
 (C.26) 
 
 Using ( C.20 ), Cauchy–Schwarz in  y ,
( C.17 ), and
( C.26 ), we obtain 
 

 
  
  
 \displaystyle\eta^{-1/2}\,\Bigl|\int\varepsilon_{y}^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr| 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt\leq C\sqrt{\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}}\,\Bigl\{\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}+\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2} 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt\leq C\sqrt{\beta\eta}\,\mathcal{R}^{1/2}\,\Bigl\{\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2}+C\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}\,. 
  
 (C.27) 
 
 
 
 Substituting these three estimates into
( C.23 ) gives 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathcal{R}+4\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) 
 \displaystyle\leq 2\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\| 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt+C\sqrt{\beta\eta}\,\mathcal{R}^{1/2}\Bigl\{\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2}+C\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}\,. 
  
 
 For sufficiently small universal  \beta\eta , the last two terms
are absorbed into the left-hand side. This proves ( C.18 ).
∎ 
 
 
 
 Proof of  Proposition   C.5 . 
 
 Choose a small enough universal constant in the hypothesis, if necessary, so
that  Lemma   C.6  applies. On
 \operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[1/2,1]}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})) , the operator
 \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}  is bounded.
Applying ( B.13 ) first to
 \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}w  and then to
 \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w  gives 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\nabla w\| 
 \displaystyle\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}w\|\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}w\|\,, 
  
 
  
 \displaystyle\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\| 
 \displaystyle\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{3}w\|\,. 
  
 (C.28) 
 
 Here, the final inequality in each line follows by spectral calculus
on  [\frac{1}{2},1] . 
 
 
 Decompose  \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w  according to
( C.15 ). The affine part has
coefficient  2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y} , so
 Proposition   C.2 ,  \|I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\|\leq 2 
applied to the error term, and  (r+s)^{2}\leq 2r^{2}+2s^{2}  give 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w\|^{2} 
 \displaystyle\leq C\beta\eta^{2}\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)+C\,\mathbb{E}[e_{Y_{+}}(W)^{2}] 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\leq C\eta\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\|\,, 
  
 
 where the last line uses  \beta\eta\leq 1  and
 Lemma   C.6 . Finally,
( C.28 ) gives ( C.14 ).
∎ 
 
 
 

A3.p1 
 This appendix establishes the two model-specific estimates used by the
modified  L^{2}  method argument of Appendix  B : the
macroscopic coercivity lemma
( Lemma   B.1 ) and the global half-turn bound
( Proposition   B.2 ). 

A3.p1.1 This appendix establishes the two model-specific estimates used by the
modified  L^{2}  method argument of Appendix  B : the
macroscopic coercivity lemma
( Lemma   B.1 ) and the global half-turn bound
( Proposition   B.2 ).
A3.SS1 
 C.1  Proof of
 Lemma   B.1 

 
 Proof of  Lemma   B.1 . 
 
 We first prove
( B.13 ). It suffices to consider
 f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d}) , the general case following by density and
closedness of the gradient. 
 
 
 Recall that the conditional
density  p_{y}  of  Y_{-}  given  Y_{+}=y  is 
 

 
  
 p_{y}(u)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V((y+u)/2)-\frac{\|y-u\|^{2}}{8\eta}\Bigr\}\,, 
  
 
 and define 
 

 
  
 s_{y}(u)\coloneqq-\frac{1}{2}\,\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\,. 
  
 
 Differentiating the normalized conditional density gives 
 

 
  
 \nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}\bigl(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\,. 
  
 (C.1) 
 
 
 
 The negative log-density of  p_{y}  has Hessian 
 

 
  
 \frac{1}{4}\,\bigl\{\nabla^{2}V((y+u)/2)+\eta^{-1}I\bigr\}\succeq\frac{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}{4}\,I\,. 
  
 
 Hence, the conditional Poincaré inequality implies, for every unit
vector  a , 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr) 
 \displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|\nabla_{u}(a^{\top}s_{y})(Y_{-})\|^{2}\,\bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\bigr]\,. 
  
 
 The assumptions
 \alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V\preceq\beta I  and
 \beta\eta\leq 1  give 
 

 
  
 \|\nabla_{u}s_{y}(u)\|_{\rm op}\leq\frac{\eta^{-1}-\alpha}{4}\,. 
  
 
 Consequently, 
 

 
  
 \Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\,. 
  
 
 Applying conditional Cauchy–Schwarz in
( C.1 ) and taking the supremum over
 \|a\|=1  yields 
 

 
  
 \|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)\|^{2}\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 Integrating in  y  and using ( B.9 ), 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{(1-\alpha\eta)^{2}}{4\,(1+\alpha\eta)}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.2) 
 
 Finally,
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|f\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2} ,
which proves ( B.13 ). 
 
 
 We next bound the spectrum of  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} . Let  f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} .
Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  preserves constants,  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f  is centered.
The Poincaré inequality for the marginal law  \pi_{\eta}^{Y}  gives 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{Y}}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)\leq\Bigl(\frac{1}{\alpha}+\eta\Bigr)\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.3) 
 
 Combining ( C.3 ) with
( C.2 ) and rearranging gives 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|\leq\frac{1-\alpha\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\|f\|=\rho_{\rm mac}\,\|f\|\,. 
  
 (C.4) 
 
 Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  is self-adjoint, this proves 
 

 
  
 -\rho_{\rm mac}I\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\preceq\rho_{\rm mac}I\,. 
  
 
 It remains only to obtain the uniform lower bound
 \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\succeq-\frac{1}{2}\,I .
For every  g\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) , exchangeability and
( B.8 ) give 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle 
 \displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\bigl(g(Y_{+})-g(Y_{-})\bigr)^{2}\bigr]=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(X+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(X-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr]\,. 
  
 (C.5) 
 
 For fixed  X=x , the Gaussian Poincaré inequality implies 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr] 
 \displaystyle\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl\|\nabla g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)+\nabla g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr\|^{2}\,. 
  
 
 Averaging in  X  and using that  Y_{+}  and  Y_{-}  have the same
marginal gives 
 

 
  
 \bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla g\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.6) 
 
 
 
 Applying ( C.6 ) with  g=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f  and using
( B.13 ), we obtain 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\bigl\langle f,\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})f\bigr\rangle 
 \displaystyle=\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl\langle f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})f\bigr\rangle\,. 
  
 
 Thus, as an operator inequality,  2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\preceq I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2} ,
or equivalently 
 

 
  
 (I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})^{2}(I+2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\succeq 0\,. 
  
 (C.7) 
 
 Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  is a self-adjoint contraction, its spectrum lies in
 [-1,1] . The scalar inequality corresponding to
( C.7 ) is 
 

 
  
 (1-\lambda)^{2}(1+2\lambda)\geq 0\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\lambda\in\operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\,, 
  
 
 and therefore  \operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\subseteq[-1/2,1] . Hence
 -\frac{1}{2}I\,\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} .
Together with ( C.4 ), this proves
( B.14 ). 
 
 
 Finally, 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\succeq(1-\rho_{\rm mac}^{2})\,I=\frac{4\alpha\eta}{(1+\alpha\eta)^{2}}\,I\,, 
  
 
 and therefore 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\frac{2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}{1+\alpha\eta}\,I=\gamma_{\rm gap}I\,. 
  
 
 This proves ( B.15 ).
∎ 
 
 
 
 The half-turn analysis below uses the following alternative form of
( C.1 ). 
 
 
 Lemma C.1   (Derivative formula for  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} ) . 
 
 For every  f\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})  and  \pi_{\eta}^{Y} -almost every  y , 
 

 
  
 \nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 (C.8) 
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 First let  f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d}) . The representation
( C.1 ) gives 
 

 
  
 \nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 Using the definition of  s_{y} , we write 
 

 
  
 s_{y}(u)=\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\Bigr\}-\nabla V((y+u)/2)\,. 
  
 
 To rewrite ( C.1 ) in the claimed form, we
only need to identify the covariance generated by the vector in braces.
Integration by parts in  u  against the conditional density  p_{y}  gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\ \Bigm|\ Y_{+}=y\Bigr]\,. 
  
 (C.9) 
 
 Applying ( C.9 ) to  f\equiv 1 , justified by compactly
supported cutoffs converging to  1 , gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}\Bigl[\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]=0\,. 
  
 
 Combining this with ( C.9 ), we obtain 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y) 
 \displaystyle=\mathrm{Cov}\Bigl(f(Y_{-}),\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr)-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y) 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y) 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 This is ( C.8 ) for smooth  f . 
 
 
 The extension to  H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})  is a standard approximation
argument once we check that the covariance term is continuous in
 L^{2} . By ( 2.11 ), the conditional
law of  X  given  Y_{+}=y  has Poincaré constant at most
 \eta/(1+\alpha\eta)\leq\eta . Hence, for every unit vector
 a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , 
 

 
  
 \Var(a^{\top}\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla^{2}V(X)\,a\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\leq\beta^{2}\eta\,. 
  
 
 Conditional Cauchy–Schwarz and
( B.9 ) therefore imply 
 

 
  
 \bigl\|\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+})\bigr\|_{L^{2}}\leq\beta\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|\,, 
  
 
 and the same bound applies to differences. Conditional expectation is
an  L^{2}  contraction, and ( B.13 ) controls the
left side. Thus every term in ( C.8 ) is
continuous under  H^{1}  approximation. Density of
 C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})  in  H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})  completes the proof.
∎ 
 
 

A3.SS1.2 
 Proof of  Lemma   B.1 . 
 
 We first prove
( B.13 ). It suffices to consider
 f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d}) , the general case following by density and
closedness of the gradient. 
 
 
 Recall that the conditional
density  p_{y}  of  Y_{-}  given  Y_{+}=y  is 
 

 
  
 p_{y}(u)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V((y+u)/2)-\frac{\|y-u\|^{2}}{8\eta}\Bigr\}\,, 
  
 
 and define 
 

 
  
 s_{y}(u)\coloneqq-\frac{1}{2}\,\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\,. 
  
 
 Differentiating the normalized conditional density gives 
 

 
  
 \nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}\bigl(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\,. 
  
 (C.1) 
 
 
 
 The negative log-density of  p_{y}  has Hessian 
 

 
  
 \frac{1}{4}\,\bigl\{\nabla^{2}V((y+u)/2)+\eta^{-1}I\bigr\}\succeq\frac{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}{4}\,I\,. 
  
 
 Hence, the conditional Poincaré inequality implies, for every unit
vector  a , 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr) 
 \displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|\nabla_{u}(a^{\top}s_{y})(Y_{-})\|^{2}\,\bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\bigr]\,. 
  
 
 The assumptions
 \alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V\preceq\beta I  and
 \beta\eta\leq 1  give 
 

 
  
 \|\nabla_{u}s_{y}(u)\|_{\rm op}\leq\frac{\eta^{-1}-\alpha}{4}\,. 
  
 
 Consequently, 
 

 
  
 \Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\,. 
  
 
 Applying conditional Cauchy–Schwarz in
( C.1 ) and taking the supremum over
 \|a\|=1  yields 
 

 
  
 \|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)\|^{2}\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. 
  
 
 Integrating in  y  and using ( B.9 ), 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{(1-\alpha\eta)^{2}}{4\,(1+\alpha\eta)}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.2) 
 
 Finally,
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|f\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2} ,
which proves ( B.13 ). 
 
 
 We next bound the spectrum of  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} . Let  f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} .
Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  preserves constants,  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f  is centered.
The Poincaré inequality for the marginal law  \pi_{\eta}^{Y}  gives 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{Y}}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)\leq\Bigl(\frac{1}{\alpha}+\eta\Bigr)\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.3) 
 
 Combining ( C.3 ) with
( C.2 ) and rearranging gives 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|\leq\frac{1-\alpha\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\|f\|=\rho_{\rm mac}\,\|f\|\,. 
  
 (C.4) 
 
 Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  is self-adjoint, this proves 
 

 
  
 -\rho_{\rm mac}I\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\preceq\rho_{\rm mac}I\,. 
  
 
 It remains only to obtain the uniform lower bound
 \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\succeq-\frac{1}{2}\,I .
For every  g\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) , exchangeability and
( B.8 ) give 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle 
 \displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\bigl(g(Y_{+})-g(Y_{-})\bigr)^{2}\bigr]=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(X+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(X-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr]\,. 
  
 (C.5) 
 
 For fixed  X=x , the Gaussian Poincaré inequality implies 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr] 
 \displaystyle\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl\|\nabla g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)+\nabla g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr\|^{2}\,. 
  
 
 Averaging in  X  and using that  Y_{+}  and  Y_{-}  have the same
marginal gives 
 

 
  
 \bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla g\|^{2}\,. 
  
 (C.6) 
 
 
 
 Applying ( C.6 ) with  g=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f  and using
( B.13 ), we obtain 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\bigl\langle f,\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})f\bigr\rangle 
 \displaystyle=\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl\langle f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})f\bigr\rangle\,. 
  
 
 Thus, as an operator inequality,  2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\preceq I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2} ,
or equivalently 
 

 
  
 (I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})^{2}(I+2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\succeq 0\,. 
  
 (C.7) 
 
 Since  \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}  is a self-adjoint contraction, its spectrum lies in
 [-1,1] . The scalar inequality corresponding to
( C.7 ) is 
 

 
  
 (1-\lambda)^{2}(1+2\lambda)\geq 0\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\lambda\in\operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\,, 
  
 
 and therefore  \operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\subseteq[-1/2,1] . Hence
 -\frac{1}{2}I\,\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} .
Together with ( C.4 ), this proves
( B.14 ). 
 
 
 Finally, 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\succeq(1-\rho_{\rm mac}^{2})\,I=\frac{4\alpha\eta}{(1+\alpha\eta)^{2}}\,I\,, 
  
 
 and therefore 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\frac{2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}{1+\alpha\eta}\,I=\gamma_{\rm gap}I\,. 
  
 
 This proves ( B.15 ).
∎ 
 

