A3.SS2.3 
 Proof. 
 
 Under  Y_{+}=y , we have  X=y+\sqrt{\eta}W  and
 Y_{-}=y+2\sqrt{\eta}W . Applying ( C.9 ) to
 f:u\mapsto F((u-y)/(2\sqrt{\eta}))  therefore gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}\nabla F(W)=\mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}[F(W)\,\{W+g_{y}(W)\}]\,. 
  
 
 Taking  F\equiv 1  and then the coordinate functions of  W  and
 g_{y}(W) , with the same cutoff approximation as in
 Lemma   C.1 , gives 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{E}g_{y}(W)=-\bar{w}_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathrm{Cov}(W,g_{y}(W))=I-\Sigma_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathbb{E}\nabla g_{y}(W)=I-\Sigma_{y}+\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))\,. 
  
 
 For every  a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , the same conditional Poincaré estimate used in
the proof of  Lemma   C.1 , together with
 (\nabla g_{y})^{2}\preceq\beta\eta\,\nabla g_{y}  then give 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle a^{\top}\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))a 
 \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{E}\|\nabla g_{y}(W)\,a\|^{2}\leq\beta\eta\,a^{\top}\{I-\Sigma_{y}+\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))\}a\,. 
  
 
 Rearranging proves ( C.13 ).
∎ 
 

