A4 
 Appendix D  Technical tools 

 
 This appendix collects the functional analysis and log-concavity tools
used throughout the process and convergence analyses. 
 
 
 D.1  Hilbert space and Markov operator conventions 

 
 Definition D.1   (Hilbert space notation) . 
 
 Let  \zeta  be a probability measure. The real Hilbert space
 L^{2}(\zeta)  consists of equivalence classes of measurable functions
 f  such that  \int f^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\zeta<\infty . Its inner product and
norm are 
 

 
  
 \langle f,g\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\int fg\,\mathrm{d}\zeta\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|f\|_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\langle f,f\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}^{1/2}\,. 
  
 (D.1) 
 
 We write 
 

 
  
 L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)\coloneqq\Bigl\{f\in L^{2}(\zeta):\int f\,\mathrm{d}\zeta=0\Bigr\}\,. 
  
 (D.2) 
 
 When  \zeta=\pi_{\eta} , we abbreviate the inner product and norm by
 \langle f,g\rangle  and  \|f\| . 
 
 
 
 Two functions equal  \zeta -almost everywhere represent the same
vector. A closed linear subspace  \mathcal{M}\subseteq L^{2}(\zeta) 
has orthogonal complement
 \mathcal{M}^{\perp}\coloneqq\{g:\langle f,g\rangle=0\text{ for all }f\in\mathcal{M}\} .
Its orthogonal projection is the unique bounded operator
 \mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}  for which  \mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}  and
 f-\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}^{\perp} . 
 
 
 All operators in the convergence analysis are bounded linear operators
on the indicated Hilbert spaces unless explicitly stated otherwise.
The adjoint  \mathsf{L}^{*}  is characterized by
 \langle\mathsf{L}f,g\rangle=\langle f,\mathsf{L}^{*}g\rangle .
An operator is self-adjoint if  \mathsf{L}=\mathsf{L}^{*} , a contraction
if  \|\mathsf{L}f\|\leq\|f\| , and unitary if it is onto and preserves
norms. A self-adjoint involution, meaning  \mathsf{L}^{2}=I , is
unitary. For self-adjoint operators  \mathsf{L}_{1},\mathsf{L}_{2} , 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{L}_{1}\preceq\mathsf{L}_{2}\hskip 10.00002pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 10.00002pt\langle f,(\mathsf{L}_{2}-\mathsf{L}_{1})f\rangle\geq 0\hskip 10.00002pt\text{for every }f 
  
 (D.3) 
 
 is the Loewner order. If  \mathsf{L}  is self-adjoint, the
spectral theorem defines  \psi(\mathsf{L})  for bounded Borel functions
 \psi ; in particular  \mathbf{1}_{E}(\mathsf{L})  is the orthogonal
projection onto the spectral set  E , and a non-negative operator has
a unique non-negative square root. 
 
 
 Definition D.2   (Markov operators and density evolution) . 
 
 Let  \mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})  be a Markov kernel preserving  \zeta . Its
Markov operator on observables is 
 

 
  
 (\mathsf{K}f)(z)\coloneqq\int f(z^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})\,. 
  
 (D.4) 
 
 It is a contraction on  L^{2}(\zeta) . Its adjoint
 \mathsf{K}^{*}  governs density evolution: if a law has density  h  with
respect to  \zeta , then its density after one step is  \mathsf{K}^{*}h .
Constants are fixed by both operators. 
 
 
 
 If  P\ll\zeta  has density  h , then 
 

 
  
 \chi^{2}(P\|\zeta)=\|h-1\|_{\zeta}^{2}\,. 
  
 (D.5) 
 
 Consequently, an operator norm bound for  \mathsf{K}  on
 L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)  gives the same bound for its adjoint
 \mathsf{K}^{*} , and hence gives  \chi^{2}  contraction. 
 
 
 
 D.2  Strong log-concavity and covariance bounds 

 
 We repeatedly use the following standard consequences of
Bakry–Émery curvature. If a probability measure
 \nu(\mathrm{d}z)\propto e^{-W(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z  satisfies
 \nabla^{2}W\succeq mI , then, for smooth  f , 
 

 
  
 \Var_{\nu}(f)\leq\frac{1}{m}\,\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\|\nabla f\|^{2}\,, 
  
 (D.6) 
 
 and  \nu  satisfies the log-Sobolev inequality with constant
 1/m . In particular, every  L -Lipschitz  f  obeys Gaussian
concentration 
 

 
  
 \mathbb{P}\{f-\mathbb{E}f\geq t\}\leq\exp\Bigl(-\frac{mt^{2}}{2L^{2}}\Bigr)\,. 
  
 (D.7) 
 
 These statements follow from the Bakry–Émery criterion; see, for
example,  [ 3 , Chapters 4 and 5] . 
 
 
 Lemma D.3   (Two-sided covariance bound) . 
 
 Let  \nu(\mathrm{d}z)\propto e^{-W(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z  with 
 

 
  
 mI\preceq\nabla^{2}W(z)\preceq LI\,. 
  
 (D.8) 
 
 Then 
 

 
  
 L^{-1}I\preceq\mathrm{Cov}_{\nu}(Z)\preceq m^{-1}I\,. 
  
 (D.9) 
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 The upper bound is the Brascamp–Lieb
inequality  [ 3 , Chapter 4]  applied to linear functions. The lower
bound follows from the Cramér–Rao inequality for the location family
generated by  \nu : 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\mathrm{Cov}_{\nu}(Z) 
 \displaystyle\succeq\bigl(\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\nabla^{2}W(Z)\bigr)^{-1}\succeq L^{-1}I\,.\qed 
  
 
 
 
 
 Hessian of the augmented marginal. 

 
 Let  \bar{x}_{y}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=y]  and
 \Sigma_{y}\coloneqq\eta^{-1}\,\mathrm{Cov}(X\mid Y=y) , as in the normalized
coordinates of Appendix  C . Differentiating the
log-density of  \pi_{\eta}^{Y}  gives 
 

 
  
 \nabla U_{\eta}(y)=\eta^{-1}(y-\bar{x}_{y})\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\nabla^{2}U_{\eta}(y)=\eta^{-1}(I-\Sigma_{y})\,. 
  
 (D.10) 
 
 The conditional potential in  x  has Hessian between
 (\alpha+\eta^{-1})I  and  (\beta+\eta^{-1})I . Applying
 Lemma   D.3  in ( D.10 ) gives
( 2.13 ). 
 
 
 
 
 D.3  Comparison of positive operators 

 
 Lemma D.4   (Comparison of positive operators) . 
 
 Let  \mathsf{A},\mathsf{B}  be bounded positive semidefinite self-adjoint
operators on a Hilbert space  \mathcal{H} , and suppose that for  M\geq m>0 ,  0<mI\preceq\mathsf{B}\preceq MI .
Assume that, for every  v\in\mathcal{H} ,  \|\mathsf{A}v\|^{2}\leq\|v\|\,\|\mathsf{B}v\| .
Then, for  K\coloneqq\frac{M}{m} , 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{A}^{2}\preceq\bigl(1+\frac{\log K}{\pi}\bigr)\,\mathsf{B}\,. 
  
 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 By replacing 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{A}\leftarrow m^{-1/2}\mathsf{A}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{B}\leftarrow m^{-1}\mathsf{B}\,, 
  
 
 we may assume without loss of generality that  I\preceq\mathsf{B}\preceq KI .
For non-zero  v\in\mathcal{H} , 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\langle v,\mathsf{A}^{2}v\rangle 
 \displaystyle=\langle\mathsf{B}^{1/2}v,\mathsf{B}^{-1/2}\mathsf{A}^{2}\mathsf{B}^{-1/2}\mathsf{B}^{1/2}v\rangle\leq\|\mathsf{B}^{-1/2}\mathsf{A}^{2}\mathsf{B}^{-1/2}\|\,\langle v,\mathsf{B}v\rangle=\|\mathsf{A}\mathsf{B}^{-1}\mathsf{A}\|\,\langle v,\mathsf{B}v\rangle\,. 
  
 
 Also, 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|\mathsf{A}\mathsf{B}^{-1}\mathsf{A}\| 
 \displaystyle=\Bigl\lVert\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathsf{A}(t^{2}I+\mathsf{B}^{2})^{-1}\mathsf{A}\,\mathrm{d}t\Bigr\rVert\leq\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\infty}\|\mathsf{A}(t^{2}I+\mathsf{B}^{2})^{-1}\mathsf{A}\|\,\mathrm{d}t\,. 
  
 
 Moreover, by hypothesis, 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|\mathsf{A}(t^{2}I+\mathsf{B}^{2})^{-1}\mathsf{A}\| 
 \displaystyle=\|(t^{2}I+\mathsf{B}^{2})^{-1/2}\mathsf{A}^{2}(t^{2}I+\mathsf{B}^{2})^{-1/2}\| 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle=\sup_{v\in\mathcal{H}\setminus\{0\}}\frac{\|\mathsf{A}(t^{2}I+\mathsf{B}^{2})^{-1/2}v\|^{2}}{\|v\|^{2}}=\sup_{u\in\mathcal{H}\setminus\{0\}}\frac{\|\mathsf{A}u\|^{2}}{t^{2}\,\|u\|^{2}+\|\mathsf{B}u\|^{2}} 
  
 
  
  
 \displaystyle\leq\sup_{u\in\mathcal{H}\setminus\{0\}}\frac{\|u\|\,\|\mathsf{B}u\|}{t^{2}\,\|u\|^{2}+\|\mathsf{B}u\|^{2}}\,. 
  
 
 Setting  r\coloneqq\|\mathsf{B}u\|/\|u\|\in[1,K] , 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|\mathsf{A}(t^{2}I+\mathsf{B}^{2})^{-1}\mathsf{A}\| 
 \displaystyle\leq\sup_{r\in[1,K]}\frac{r}{t^{2}+r^{2}}=\begin{cases}1/(t^{2}+1)\,,&t<1\,,\\
1/(2t)\,,&t\in[1,K]\,,\\
K/(t^{2}+K^{2})\,,&t>K\,.\end{cases} 
  
 
 Hence, 
 

 
  
 \displaystyle\|\mathsf{A}\mathsf{B}^{-1}\mathsf{A}\| 
 \displaystyle\leq\frac{2}{\pi}\,\Bigl(\int_{0}^{1}\frac{1}{t^{2}+1}\,\mathrm{d}t+\int_{1}^{K}\frac{1}{2t}\,\mathrm{d}t+\int_{K}^{\infty}\frac{K}{t^{2}+K^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}t\Bigr)=1+\frac{\log K}{\pi}\,. 
  
 
 This completes the proof.
∎ 
 
 
 
 The logarithmic dependence on  K  in  Lemma   D.4  is sharp in general. 
 
 
 Remark D.5 . 
 
 Let  K>1 , take  \mathcal{H}=L^{2}([1,K],\mathrm{d}x) , and  \mathsf{A}f(x)\coloneqq(\pi\,(K-1))^{-1/2}\int_{1}^{K}f(y)\,\mathrm{d}y ,  \mathsf{B}f(x)\coloneqq xf(x) .
Then, by the classical integral Carlson inequality, 
 

 
  
 \|\mathsf{A}f\|^{2}=\frac{1}{\pi}\,\Bigl(\int_{1}^{K}f(y)\,\mathrm{d}y\Bigr)^{2}\leq\|f\|\,\|\mathsf{B}f\|\,. 
  
 
 Thus, the hypotheses of  Lemma   D.4  are met.
If we take  f(x)\coloneqq 1/x , then  \|\mathsf{A}f\|^{2}=(\log K)^{2}/\pi , whereas  \langle f,\mathsf{B}f\rangle=\log K , so the best possible constant in  Lemma   D.4  can be no better than  \max\{1,(\log K)/\pi\} , where the maximum with  1  arises from the trivial case  \mathsf{A}=\mathsf{B}=I . 
 
 
 

A4.p1 
 This appendix collects the functional analysis and log-concavity tools
used throughout the process and convergence analyses. 

A4.p1.1 This appendix collects the functional analysis and log-concavity tools
used throughout the process and convergence analyses.
A4.SS1 
 D.1  Hilbert space and Markov operator conventions 

 
 Definition D.1   (Hilbert space notation) . 
 
 Let  \zeta  be a probability measure. The real Hilbert space
 L^{2}(\zeta)  consists of equivalence classes of measurable functions
 f  such that  \int f^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\zeta<\infty . Its inner product and
norm are 
 

 
  
 \langle f,g\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\int fg\,\mathrm{d}\zeta\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|f\|_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\langle f,f\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}^{1/2}\,. 
  
 (D.1) 
 
 We write 
 

 
  
 L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)\coloneqq\Bigl\{f\in L^{2}(\zeta):\int f\,\mathrm{d}\zeta=0\Bigr\}\,. 
  
 (D.2) 
 
 When  \zeta=\pi_{\eta} , we abbreviate the inner product and norm by
 \langle f,g\rangle  and  \|f\| . 
 
 
 
 Two functions equal  \zeta -almost everywhere represent the same
vector. A closed linear subspace  \mathcal{M}\subseteq L^{2}(\zeta) 
has orthogonal complement
 \mathcal{M}^{\perp}\coloneqq\{g:\langle f,g\rangle=0\text{ for all }f\in\mathcal{M}\} .
Its orthogonal projection is the unique bounded operator
 \mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}  for which  \mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}  and
 f-\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}^{\perp} . 
 
 
 All operators in the convergence analysis are bounded linear operators
on the indicated Hilbert spaces unless explicitly stated otherwise.
The adjoint  \mathsf{L}^{*}  is characterized by
 \langle\mathsf{L}f,g\rangle=\langle f,\mathsf{L}^{*}g\rangle .
An operator is self-adjoint if  \mathsf{L}=\mathsf{L}^{*} , a contraction
if  \|\mathsf{L}f\|\leq\|f\| , and unitary if it is onto and preserves
norms. A self-adjoint involution, meaning  \mathsf{L}^{2}=I , is
unitary. For self-adjoint operators  \mathsf{L}_{1},\mathsf{L}_{2} , 
 

 
  
 \mathsf{L}_{1}\preceq\mathsf{L}_{2}\hskip 10.00002pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 10.00002pt\langle f,(\mathsf{L}_{2}-\mathsf{L}_{1})f\rangle\geq 0\hskip 10.00002pt\text{for every }f 
  
 (D.3) 
 
 is the Loewner order. If  \mathsf{L}  is self-adjoint, the
spectral theorem defines  \psi(\mathsf{L})  for bounded Borel functions
 \psi ; in particular  \mathbf{1}_{E}(\mathsf{L})  is the orthogonal
projection onto the spectral set  E , and a non-negative operator has
a unique non-negative square root. 
 
 
 Definition D.2   (Markov operators and density evolution) . 
 
 Let  \mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})  be a Markov kernel preserving  \zeta . Its
Markov operator on observables is 
 

 
  
 (\mathsf{K}f)(z)\coloneqq\int f(z^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})\,. 
  
 (D.4) 
 
 It is a contraction on  L^{2}(\zeta) . Its adjoint
 \mathsf{K}^{*}  governs density evolution: if a law has density  h  with
respect to  \zeta , then its density after one step is  \mathsf{K}^{*}h .
Constants are fixed by both operators. 
 
 
 
 If  P\ll\zeta  has density  h , then 
 

 
  
 \chi^{2}(P\|\zeta)=\|h-1\|_{\zeta}^{2}\,. 
  
 (D.5) 
 
 Consequently, an operator norm bound for  \mathsf{K}  on
 L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)  gives the same bound for its adjoint
 \mathsf{K}^{*} , and hence gives  \chi^{2}  contraction. 
 

